Okay.

00:11:37.083 --> 00:11:39.041
Lone Pine, California is like,

00:11:39.041 --> 00:11:43.700
if you believe, there's
like the film museum there

00:11:43.700 --> 00:11:46.070
because they've shot so
many things in Lone Pine

00:11:46.070 --> 00:11:51.070
because the mountains can
look like the Himalayas.

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:54.280
So they've shot the
mountains in California,

00:11:54.280 --> 00:11:56.160
right there next to Lone
Pine as the Himalayas

00:11:56.160 --> 00:11:57.460
in all kinds of movies.

00:11:57.460 --> 00:11:58.486
Oh.

00:11:58.486 --> 00:11:59.770
Talkin' about like Mount
Whitney and all that?

00:11:59.770 --> 00:12:01.660
Yeah, and if you go by, you know,

00:12:01.660 --> 00:12:04.150
'cause we go to Mammoth to
ski and as you take that trip,

00:12:04.150 --> 00:12:04.983
a lot of times you're like,

00:12:04.983 --> 00:12:06.550
man, that looks like the Himalayas.

00:12:06.550 --> 00:12:08.870
Somebody who doesn't know
what the Himalayas look like.

00:12:08.870 --> 00:12:09.703
Right.

00:12:10.580 --> 00:12:13.067
But there's just so many movies
that have been shot there.

00:12:13.067 --> 00:12:14.200
And this Alabama Hills,

00:12:14.200 --> 00:12:15.220
a bunch of movies have been shot there

00:12:15.220 --> 00:12:19.460
'cause it has like a
Martian kind of, you know,

00:12:19.460 --> 00:12:21.610
just sort of this
different sort of landscape

00:12:21.610 --> 00:12:22.670
sort of feel to it.

00:12:22.670 --> 00:12:26.380
Anyway, so I get there
and it was one of those

00:12:27.470 --> 00:12:29.440
remote, but not completely remote.

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:32.083
Like the park is,

00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:36.450
you know, it's a few
square miles or whatever,

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:38.690
but there's just a bunch of dirt roads

00:12:38.690 --> 00:12:40.920
that go up to these big boulders

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:42.070
and there'll be a fire ring there.

00:12:42.070 --> 00:12:44.774
And I'm like driving around
like, ah, somebody there.

00:12:44.774 --> 00:12:46.077
I'm gonna keep going.

00:12:46.077 --> 00:12:47.620
Oh, somebody at this one.

00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:49.640
And eventually I found one
that somebody wasn't at

00:12:49.640 --> 00:12:54.100
and it was like a giant sort of cul-de-sac

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:58.830
with this big rock and
then this rock wall,

00:12:58.830 --> 00:13:01.370
kind of U shaped rock wall that
had like a name on the map,

00:13:01.370 --> 00:13:02.890
it was like for rock climbers.

00:13:02.890 --> 00:13:06.410
I think it was called like the
Loaf or something like that.

00:13:06.410 --> 00:13:10.600
It's like seven, eight stories
tall of these giant rocks.

00:13:10.600 --> 00:13:12.110
And so I was like, okay, I'm here.

00:13:12.110 --> 00:13:16.610
Like I can, like, if the person
at the camp site next to me,

00:13:16.610 --> 00:13:19.430
turns their car on, I can hear that.

00:13:19.430 --> 00:13:20.960
And if they yell, I can hear that,

00:13:20.960 --> 00:13:24.590
but I can't see them because
they're over this like rock

00:13:24.590 --> 00:13:25.630
sort of facade.
That's good.

00:13:25.630 --> 00:13:27.860
No line of sight, that's what you want.

00:13:27.860 --> 00:13:29.900
And I can like play music at my campsite

00:13:29.900 --> 00:13:31.272
and they can't really hear it,

00:13:31.272 --> 00:13:32.105
like it'd have to be pretty loud.

00:13:32.105 --> 00:13:32.938
Yeah.

00:13:32.938 --> 00:13:35.190
So I was like, it turns out
that that is the sweet spot

00:13:35.190 --> 00:13:38.080
as I will get to when you
are too close to somebody

00:13:38.080 --> 00:13:40.010
or when you're too far away from somebody,

00:13:40.010 --> 00:13:42.110
you get a whole different problem.

00:13:42.110 --> 00:13:43.870
Which I did for two nights,
I'll talk about that.

00:13:43.870 --> 00:13:48.720
But that first day I like parked the car,

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:50.730
get the rooftop tent opened up,

00:13:50.730 --> 00:13:53.601
get everything set up,
put my little table out.

00:13:53.601 --> 00:13:56.923
And then I just, like, I'm
gonna climb these rocks.

00:13:58.020 --> 00:13:59.580
Why not, right?

00:13:59.580 --> 00:14:02.060
And I just start, I go up
to the rocks and I just,

00:14:02.060 --> 00:14:04.100
first of all, I'm thinking
about snakes constantly.

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:05.920
It's amazing how the older you get,

00:14:05.920 --> 00:14:08.760
the more you think about all
the things that could go wrong.

00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:10.467
When I was a child--

00:14:10.467 --> 00:14:13.390
You're gettin' an enema from a snake.

00:14:13.390 --> 00:14:14.970
Well, that's not how it works.

00:14:14.970 --> 00:14:17.080
As a child, you know, as a kid growing up

00:14:17.080 --> 00:14:18.390
we're just like swimming
in the Cape Fear River,

00:14:18.390 --> 00:14:21.270
stepping on water moccasins,
not thinking about anything.

00:14:21.270 --> 00:14:24.770
And I'm like walking
along and like clapping.

00:14:24.770 --> 00:14:26.680
I'm like, they can hear clapping, right?

00:14:26.680 --> 00:14:27.880
Stamping really hard, if
somebody was to see me,

00:14:27.880 --> 00:14:29.440
like, what is he trying to do?

00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:30.610
I think they hear heat.

00:14:30.610 --> 00:14:33.870
No they hear vibrations, so
I'm trying to make vibrations.

00:14:33.870 --> 00:14:35.670
'Cause I know it's gonna
be a rattlesnake probably

00:14:35.670 --> 00:14:37.180
if I see one.

00:14:37.180 --> 00:14:39.150
And I'm like, are they
still out this time of year?

00:14:39.150 --> 00:14:40.850
I think so, it's still pretty hot.

00:14:42.440 --> 00:14:44.560
But eventually I climb up,

00:14:44.560 --> 00:14:47.870
I get to the almost
freaking top of this thing

00:14:47.870 --> 00:14:50.070
and there's a little
place to pass through.

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:53.260
And I pass through and come
into this other valley of,

00:14:53.260 --> 00:14:57.170
there was nothing but these giant rocks

00:14:57.170 --> 00:14:59.040
and I was like, this is wonderful.

00:14:59.040 --> 00:15:01.230
What?
This is like a cathedral.

00:15:01.230 --> 00:15:03.290
And I just sat down on a rock.

00:15:03.290 --> 00:15:04.870
You passed through to the other side.

00:15:04.870 --> 00:15:07.345
And I just sat there and I was like,

00:15:07.345 --> 00:15:10.000
this is going to be a good trip.

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:13.260
But it doesn't have to be,
'cause I don't want an agenda.

00:15:13.260 --> 00:15:14.450
Did you have water?

00:15:14.450 --> 00:15:15.780
I'd have been thinking, I need water.

00:15:15.780 --> 00:15:17.810
I mean, I was still,

00:15:17.810 --> 00:15:20.080
I was 200 yards from my car at that point.

00:15:20.080 --> 00:15:22.083
Like it was, I had just gone up the rocks

00:15:22.083 --> 00:15:24.162
so I didn't have
anything, I had my camera.

00:15:24.162 --> 00:15:25.560
Were you talking to yourself yet?

00:15:25.560 --> 00:15:27.130
A little bit, whispering maybe.

00:15:27.130 --> 00:15:28.090
Oh.

00:15:28.090 --> 00:15:29.750
Because I thought people
might be able to hear me.

00:15:29.750 --> 00:15:31.050
Okay.

00:15:31.050 --> 00:15:35.850
But I go back down, I
cook myself some dinner.

00:15:35.850 --> 00:15:40.850
My food situation was a
lot of those pre-packaged,

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:43.500
just add water.

00:15:43.500 --> 00:15:44.333
Okay.

00:15:44.333 --> 00:15:46.480
Because I was like, the cleanup is minimal

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:49.580
and you're really hungry

00:15:49.580 --> 00:15:51.000
and everything tastes
good when you're camping

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:53.590
so maybe I'll be able
to get away with this.

00:15:53.590 --> 00:15:55.350
Survival packs.

00:15:55.350 --> 00:15:57.900
And what I did is before
the trip I was like,

00:15:57.900 --> 00:15:59.820
I'm gonna try a bunch of different brands.

00:15:59.820 --> 00:16:00.950
So I got, you know, Mountain House,

00:16:00.950 --> 00:16:02.420
that's the one everybody
knows about, right,

00:16:02.420 --> 00:16:04.420
that's that's the super popular one.

00:16:04.420 --> 00:16:06.062
And then there's another one

00:16:06.062 --> 00:16:09.144
that I ended up liking the
most, just if you're interested.

00:16:09.144 --> 00:16:11.860
I think it's called Packet
Gourmet or something,

00:16:11.860 --> 00:16:13.280
it's got the story of like

00:16:13.280 --> 00:16:15.600
the couple that started it in the 70s

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:17.930
and now they've got kids
and they all work there.

00:16:17.930 --> 00:16:19.933
They make some really good grits.

00:16:21.290 --> 00:16:23.420
You know, when you put water in something

00:16:23.420 --> 00:16:24.810
that's has got meat in
it sometimes it's like,

00:16:24.810 --> 00:16:25.760
man, this is like meat

00:16:25.760 --> 00:16:28.000
that could have been around until 2050.

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:28.833
Yeah.

00:16:28.833 --> 00:16:30.047
That can't be great for you

00:16:30.047 --> 00:16:34.760
and it definitely isn't great
tasting, but the grits, man.

00:16:34.760 --> 00:16:36.650
They got a grit, they got multiple grits.

00:16:36.650 --> 00:16:38.080
Packet, Packet People, Packet House?

00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:40.830
Yeah, yeah, Packet Gourmet, I don't know.

00:16:40.830 --> 00:16:42.070
Anyway, I had one of those, I was like,

00:16:42.070 --> 00:16:42.910
Hmm, it's pretty good.

00:16:42.910 --> 00:16:45.060
And then the sun's about
to set at this point.

00:16:45.060 --> 00:16:49.080
Sun's going down, I take out my journal

00:16:49.080 --> 00:16:51.610
and I decided I'm gonna
do the handwritten thing

00:16:51.610 --> 00:16:53.750
even though I'm probably
gonna get hand cramps,

00:16:53.750 --> 00:16:55.050
'cause that happens to me.

00:16:56.768 --> 00:16:58.428
Rattlesnakes and hand cramps.

00:16:58.428 --> 00:17:01.029
I Don't wanna be using--
How's he gonna survive?

00:17:01.029 --> 00:17:03.130
Or the computer because it just feels,

00:17:03.130 --> 00:17:05.360
it doesn't feel natural enough, right.

00:17:05.360 --> 00:17:07.270
So I got my journal and
I was starting to write

00:17:07.270 --> 00:17:09.200
and just kind of making some observations.

00:17:09.200 --> 00:17:11.490
Dear journal, day one.

00:17:11.490 --> 00:17:14.340
Quick review, I'll give a quick
review of the rooftop tent

00:17:14.340 --> 00:17:16.570
and then we'll take a little
break and we'll come back

00:17:16.570 --> 00:17:17.790
and I'll tell you about the first morning

00:17:17.790 --> 00:17:19.723
and how that kind of set the stage.

00:17:22.920 --> 00:17:27.060
Rooftop tent, definitely an improvement

00:17:28.550 --> 00:17:32.823
over a regular tent, in
terms of like sleep quality.

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:36.910
And also just like feeling of safety.

00:17:36.910 --> 00:17:39.080
Like you're on top of this
car and there's a ladder.

00:17:39.080 --> 00:17:41.730
I mean, I guess like a bear
could climb a ladder but.

00:17:42.920 --> 00:17:46.400
You just feel like, you
feel a little bit safer

00:17:46.400 --> 00:17:48.080
and you're literally elevated.

00:17:48.080 --> 00:17:50.040
In the circus they do a lot of things.

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:51.840
They gotta be trained to do a ladder,

00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:53.170
I think they do a tree naturally

00:17:53.170 --> 00:17:55.790
but you gotta train 'em for a ladder.

00:17:55.790 --> 00:17:57.710
It's quick, you open that thing up.

00:17:57.710 --> 00:18:02.510
It's not as quick as I wanted it to be.

00:18:02.510 --> 00:18:05.093
So it's actually pretty quick to set up.

00:18:05.990 --> 00:18:08.520
'Cause you've got this big
leather cover basically,

00:18:08.520 --> 00:18:10.250
rubber slash leather, whatever it is,

00:18:10.250 --> 00:18:13.000
and it's completely
zipped around three edges

00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:15.560
and you have to unzip it, fold it down.

00:18:15.560 --> 00:18:18.726
Then you got to open it up,
stick the ladder in there.

00:18:18.726 --> 00:18:22.190
And then you gotta, if
you want like airflow,

00:18:22.190 --> 00:18:23.370
which the first night it was kind of hot,

00:18:23.370 --> 00:18:27.000
you gotta like put these supports,

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:30.710
like these metal supports that
go into the side of the thing

00:18:30.710 --> 00:18:33.833
and then they kind of like
make the awnings taught.

00:18:34.920 --> 00:18:37.930
And I was like, I think this
took as long as a regular tent.

00:18:37.930 --> 00:18:39.600
Like I think that the
way they make tents now,

00:18:39.600 --> 00:18:42.460
which are like, blurp,
and they're basically up,

00:18:42.460 --> 00:18:45.240
I think this didn't take any less time.

00:18:45.240 --> 00:18:47.530
But there's a mattress on
the whole bottom of it.

00:18:47.530 --> 00:18:49.400
There's a mattress on the bottom of it,

00:18:49.400 --> 00:18:53.490
plus I added an air mattress
on top of that mattress.

00:18:53.490 --> 00:18:56.260
That's stored in there
or are you added it?

00:18:56.260 --> 00:18:58.020
Yeah, I mean you leave it in there

00:18:58.020 --> 00:19:01.710
and it's a self-inflating mattress

00:19:01.710 --> 00:19:03.550
and then I just kind of
deflate it every morning

00:19:03.550 --> 00:19:06.700
and then just fold it back
up in the tent, no problem.

00:19:06.700 --> 00:19:09.300
But definitely, I mean, I
definitely recommend that.

00:19:10.480 --> 00:19:11.720
But you gotta get it level.

00:19:11.720 --> 00:19:12.820
And like I was saying,

00:19:13.720 --> 00:19:16.980
I took those RV blocks and made sure

00:19:16.980 --> 00:19:18.320
it got level every single night.

00:19:18.320 --> 00:19:20.174
Did you have a level?

00:19:20.174 --> 00:19:22.590
I thought about it.
Like a bubble level?

00:19:22.590 --> 00:19:23.910
I just used my intuition.

00:19:23.910 --> 00:19:26.440
Does this feel level,
does this look level?

00:19:26.440 --> 00:19:27.993
Is this level?

00:19:27.993 --> 00:19:30.160
And it ended up being level, by that.

00:19:30.160 --> 00:19:31.980
Am I self-inflating?

00:19:31.980 --> 00:19:34.203
I overinflated the first night.

00:19:35.630 --> 00:19:36.540
You don't wanna over inflate.

00:19:36.540 --> 00:19:38.270
I wanted it to be hard

00:19:38.270 --> 00:19:40.200
because when you get an
air mattress that's soft

00:19:40.200 --> 00:19:41.230
and you wake up in the middle night

00:19:41.230 --> 00:19:45.693
and your booty or your hip bone
is like touching the bottom.

00:19:48.080 --> 00:19:49.510
You went too hard yet then.

00:19:49.510 --> 00:19:50.493
I went too hard.

00:19:51.496 --> 00:19:53.431
I was like, man, I didn't
think I could go this hard,

00:19:53.431 --> 00:19:55.730
but I went way too hard and overinflated.

00:19:55.730 --> 00:19:57.933
But I pretty much got
it right the second day.

00:19:59.070 --> 00:20:00.003
Okay, I'll get into talking about--

00:20:00.003 --> 00:20:01.580
You didn't get scared that night?

00:20:01.580 --> 00:20:03.240
Didn't get scared that first night, again,

00:20:03.240 --> 00:20:07.220
because I kind of knew that
there was somebody close by,

00:20:07.220 --> 00:20:10.010
there was a person close by
and I want to talk about that.

00:20:10.010 --> 00:20:10.843
Okay.

00:20:10.843 --> 00:20:12.240
In a little bit about why is it

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:14.434
that that brings so much comfort.

00:20:14.434 --> 00:20:17.800
But first, let's talk
about these mugs here.

00:20:17.800 --> 00:20:20.650
Mythical.com has everything you could want

00:20:20.650 --> 00:20:23.320
that has anything to do with us and you,

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:25.580
because that's what this is about.

00:20:25.580 --> 00:20:30.580
It's about you and me and
him having the same stuff.

00:20:31.780 --> 00:20:34.530
You wanna drink like us?
Having the same stuff.

00:20:34.530 --> 00:20:36.520
Then drink out of this.

00:20:36.520 --> 00:20:38.050
It's a Good Mythical Morning mug,

00:20:38.050 --> 00:20:39.750
there's different, there's a third color.

00:20:39.750 --> 00:20:42.030
Yeah, there's a third
color that's blue I believe

00:20:42.030 --> 00:20:44.295
that we don't even have right
now, but you could have it.

00:20:44.295 --> 00:20:45.860
You could be drinking out of your blue mug

00:20:45.860 --> 00:20:47.790
while we drink out of our
green and orange mugs.

00:20:47.790 --> 00:20:48.630
Connect with us.

00:20:48.630 --> 00:20:49.970
We'd be a trilogy.

00:20:49.970 --> 00:20:54.950
It makes you feel like
we're just over the ridge.

00:20:54.950 --> 00:20:56.970
You can't see us but if
we cranked up our car,

00:20:56.970 --> 00:20:58.640
you could hear us.
Right.

00:20:58.640 --> 00:21:01.130
If we slurped on our mugs.

00:21:01.130 --> 00:21:03.720
I wasn't that close, slurping
woulda not come through.

00:21:03.720 --> 00:21:05.204
You would have felt it.

00:21:05.204 --> 00:21:06.037
It would have to have been like a slurp

00:21:06.037 --> 00:21:07.192
and a choke and a cough.

00:21:07.192 --> 00:21:08.611
Only if you also own one of these.

00:21:08.611 --> 00:21:09.750
You have to slurp, choke, and cough

00:21:09.750 --> 00:21:11.280
and then I would be able to hear you.

00:21:11.280 --> 00:21:12.980
Mythical.com
Almost dropped it.

00:21:14.170 --> 00:21:15.763
Okay, so first morning,

00:21:16.900 --> 00:21:18.810
again, I wake up and I'm like,

00:21:18.810 --> 00:21:21.360
I don't know exactly
where I'm gonna go tonight

00:21:21.360 --> 00:21:23.960
because of the spring
that I had designated

00:21:23.960 --> 00:21:27.380
as my potential first
stop for a hot spring

00:21:27.380 --> 00:21:29.010
is in the national forest,

00:21:29.010 --> 00:21:31.750
is deep in the Inyo national
forest, which is closed.

00:21:31.750 --> 00:21:34.150
So I mean, are they really enforcing this?

00:21:34.150 --> 00:21:35.380
Like, what, am I gonna get fined

00:21:35.380 --> 00:21:38.593
or am I gonna get arrested,
what is it stake here?

00:21:40.300 --> 00:21:42.120
But I was like, you know what.

00:21:42.120 --> 00:21:44.190
It's not the right thing to do.

00:21:44.190 --> 00:21:45.700
Let's set that aside right now

00:21:45.700 --> 00:21:48.050
and I'm just going to sit
here and I'll tell you,

00:21:48.050 --> 00:21:51.310
the most wonderful moments
for me on this trip

00:21:52.320 --> 00:21:55.970
was waking up, and first of
all, waking up pretty early

00:21:55.970 --> 00:21:56.860
because I went to bed early.

00:21:56.860 --> 00:21:59.190
Like you get out there, especially
when you're by yourself.

00:21:59.190 --> 00:22:01.860
I mean, it gets dark and
you're kind of like, well,

00:22:01.860 --> 00:22:03.430
I guess I'll go to sleep, you know?

00:22:03.430 --> 00:22:05.310
It's like, you're just like
a farmer in the old days.

00:22:05.310 --> 00:22:07.750
It's like every human before electricity.

00:22:07.750 --> 00:22:08.720
You get dark and you're like,

00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:10.270
well, what do you do now, George?

00:22:10.270 --> 00:22:11.103
You go to sleep!

00:22:11.103 --> 00:22:13.010
Did you have the stars
like I was talking about?

00:22:13.010 --> 00:22:14.472
Oh yeah.

00:22:14.472 --> 00:22:18.920
You told me you got a
special star gazing tent,

00:22:18.920 --> 00:22:19.860
I mean seat.

00:22:19.860 --> 00:22:20.693
I did.

00:22:20.693 --> 00:22:22.950
That was the main thing that I said.

00:22:22.950 --> 00:22:25.570
I told Christie, I was
like, we've gotta invest

00:22:25.570 --> 00:22:28.000
in some camp chairs
that are actually good.

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:29.970
Because those like, those cheap ones

00:22:29.970 --> 00:22:31.260
that you get from Dick's Sporting Goods

00:22:31.260 --> 00:22:36.260
and they just wrap up, they're
the worst for your posture.

00:22:36.330 --> 00:22:37.163
And you definitely can't see the stars.

00:22:37.163 --> 00:22:38.880
Let me tell you about
these anti-gravity chairs,

00:22:38.880 --> 00:22:40.080
as they call it.

00:22:40.080 --> 00:22:42.010
It's fricking huge.

00:22:42.010 --> 00:22:44.090
And I was like, I could
take this by myself,

00:22:44.090 --> 00:22:45.840
but I couldn't take another person.

00:22:45.840 --> 00:22:46.750
It couldn't take two of these.

00:22:46.750 --> 00:22:48.770
You couldn't take, oh gosh.

00:22:48.770 --> 00:22:51.040
It was by far, it was the
biggest thing in my truck

00:22:51.040 --> 00:22:52.250
was that damn chair.

00:22:52.250 --> 00:22:54.600
I had convinced myself
that it was worth it.

00:22:54.600 --> 00:22:56.560
I think it might be and
I'll talk about that

00:22:56.560 --> 00:22:57.393
because I think night four

00:22:57.393 --> 00:22:59.020
is when I really used it to full effect

00:22:59.020 --> 00:23:00.360
to try to connect with aliens.

00:23:00.360 --> 00:23:01.213
Go ahead.

00:23:02.150 --> 00:23:05.710
But that first morning, had
my breakfast, got my coffee.

00:23:05.710 --> 00:23:07.740
That Starbucks instant coffee.

00:23:07.740 --> 00:23:08.980
The Via.

00:23:08.980 --> 00:23:10.900
I mean, I tell ya, it's good, man.

00:23:10.900 --> 00:23:11.733
We got some of that.

00:23:11.733 --> 00:23:13.250
You don't even really know

00:23:13.250 --> 00:23:14.560
that you're drinking instant coffee.

00:23:14.560 --> 00:23:15.393
Yeah, it's good.

00:23:15.393 --> 00:23:19.800
So I'm having a little coffee
and then I'm full, you know,

00:23:19.800 --> 00:23:21.610
I'm full in my belly.

00:23:21.610 --> 00:23:25.810
And again, I go back to the
journal and I just start,

00:23:25.810 --> 00:23:27.070
again, I'm not going to talk about

00:23:27.070 --> 00:23:30.560
what I was thinking about,
but again, my theme,

00:23:30.560 --> 00:23:35.560
my goal on the trip was
to be like water, right?

00:23:35.660 --> 00:23:38.370
To go with the flow, to let go,

00:23:38.370 --> 00:23:40.670
to not be this guy that
has to like analyze

00:23:40.670 --> 00:23:43.168
and differentiate and categorize

00:23:43.168 --> 00:23:45.740
and analyze every single
thing that encountering

00:23:45.740 --> 00:23:47.040
and know exactly where I'm gonna go.

00:23:47.040 --> 00:23:49.440
It was, be like water, you know,

00:23:49.440 --> 00:23:53.187
you face an obstacle and
you, you just go around it.

00:23:53.187 --> 00:23:54.937
That's the sound of water.
Yeah.

00:23:56.640 --> 00:23:57.473
I feel that.

00:23:57.473 --> 00:24:00.720
I had this really intense

00:24:02.940 --> 00:24:05.480
sort of sense that that was happening

00:24:05.480 --> 00:24:08.603
even that first morning,
like it was pretty powerful.

00:24:10.496 --> 00:24:11.550
'Cause you gave yourself permission

00:24:11.550 --> 00:24:13.910
to not just get on with anything.

00:24:13.910 --> 00:24:16.790
It wasn't about getting to the next thing.

00:24:16.790 --> 00:24:17.890
Yeah, you sat there and like,

00:24:17.890 --> 00:24:20.050
I have space right here right now.

00:24:20.050 --> 00:24:21.520
But the whole time,

00:24:21.520 --> 00:24:24.070
and this was a challenge
throughout the whole trip.

00:24:25.600 --> 00:24:27.727
When you're somebody who's not like water

00:24:27.727 --> 00:24:29.810
but you're trying to be like water

00:24:29.810 --> 00:24:34.130
the consistent challenge
when every single,

00:24:34.130 --> 00:24:38.600
every single aspect of your
trip is TBD every single day.

00:24:38.600 --> 00:24:41.170
Am I gonna run out of gas,
am I gonna get a flat tire?

00:24:41.170 --> 00:24:43.320
Am I gonna really be
able to change the tire?

00:24:43.320 --> 00:24:46.513
You know, like, am I gonna be
out of service, am I gonna?

00:24:47.610 --> 00:24:48.820
When I get to this hot spring,

00:24:48.820 --> 00:24:51.150
are there gonna be people there,
are they gonna have COVID?

00:24:51.150 --> 00:24:53.320
You know, and this is how my brain works.

00:24:53.320 --> 00:24:57.110
I'm actually considerably more anxious

00:24:57.110 --> 00:25:01.820
than I ever realized or even
sort of put off to people,

00:25:01.820 --> 00:25:03.330
you know what I mean?

00:25:03.330 --> 00:25:07.690
I'm constantly in the future,
I'm constantly thinking about

00:25:08.580 --> 00:25:13.217
all possible options,
decisions that can be made.

00:25:13.217 --> 00:25:16.520
And the whole point of the
trip was to just be present.

00:25:16.520 --> 00:25:18.990
The first thing that
I wrote in my journal,

00:25:18.990 --> 00:25:21.313
which I just did digitally.

00:25:22.150 --> 00:25:23.360
You know, some people, whatever it takes.

00:25:23.360 --> 00:25:24.193
On the first night--

00:25:24.193 --> 00:25:25.030
You probably didn't get hand cramps.

00:25:25.030 --> 00:25:27.350
First night, the thing that I wrote down,

00:25:27.350 --> 00:25:29.858
it might've been the only
thing I wrote down was,

00:25:29.858 --> 00:25:34.800
it is hard not to think about the future

00:25:34.800 --> 00:25:38.420
because even five minutes
from now is the future.

00:25:38.420 --> 00:25:41.170
So including that, I just,

00:25:41.170 --> 00:25:44.120
that was the first thing I
was over overwhelmed with.

00:25:44.120 --> 00:25:45.560
The first thought was,

00:25:45.560 --> 00:25:48.430
it's really hard to not
think about the future.

00:25:48.430 --> 00:25:52.020
I was, and exactly for that reason,

00:25:52.020 --> 00:25:55.330
because we were in control of our trips

00:25:55.330 --> 00:25:58.130
and there's always a decision to be made

00:25:58.130 --> 00:26:01.200
and you can make a better
decision if you anticipate,

00:26:01.200 --> 00:26:05.660
or if you have a plan or
even if you're letting go of,

00:26:05.660 --> 00:26:10.363
of checking certain boxes
or achieving something

00:26:11.280 --> 00:26:14.900
or realizing the perfect trip

00:26:14.900 --> 00:26:17.560
that you've visualized
in your mind, you know.

00:26:17.560 --> 00:26:20.110
Even if you let go of all that,

00:26:20.110 --> 00:26:24.690
your mind still goes to
something else and it's,

00:26:24.690 --> 00:26:26.660
for me too, it was in the future.

00:26:26.660 --> 00:26:28.930
Maybe for some people it's in the past

00:26:28.930 --> 00:26:30.850
if they're dealing with
something, but for,

00:26:30.850 --> 00:26:32.300
we're very alike in that way.

00:26:33.230 --> 00:26:35.270
Yeah, so that was a constant challenge,

00:26:35.270 --> 00:26:37.350
but it also was very evident to me

00:26:37.350 --> 00:26:38.670
'cause I started thinking things like,

00:26:38.670 --> 00:26:42.020
maybe next time I'll just go to a cabin.

00:26:42.020 --> 00:26:43.330
I'll just drive to a cabin

00:26:43.330 --> 00:26:45.160
and I'll be in the same
place the whole time.

00:26:45.160 --> 00:26:46.920
But then it was like,
no, no, no, no, no, no,

00:26:46.920 --> 00:26:48.690
don't lose the opportunity here.

00:26:48.690 --> 00:26:51.940
It's just starting.
The opportunity is to

00:26:51.940 --> 00:26:53.640
embrace the fact that you don't know

00:26:53.640 --> 00:26:54.670
where you're gonna sleep.

00:26:54.670 --> 00:26:57.730
Like that's why you're here, man.

00:26:57.730 --> 00:26:59.210
That's how you get out of the future

00:26:59.210 --> 00:27:00.350
and get into the present,

00:27:00.350 --> 00:27:02.790
is by actually being faced with--

00:27:02.790 --> 00:27:06.010
The feeling of not knowing
and not going to know.

00:27:06.010 --> 00:27:09.200
So one of the first unknowns was,

00:27:09.200 --> 00:27:11.700
okay, if I can't go to this hot spring

00:27:11.700 --> 00:27:13.780
that I wanted to go to, where can I go?

00:27:13.780 --> 00:27:16.400
Because there's another
one that I could go to,

00:27:16.400 --> 00:27:18.610
but I've already been told
basically by the internet

00:27:18.610 --> 00:27:20.780
that it's gonna be busy,
there's gonna be people there.

00:27:20.780 --> 00:27:24.040
But I was like, screw it,
I mean, it's a hot spring,

00:27:24.040 --> 00:27:25.730
let's just go check it out.

00:27:25.730 --> 00:27:28.320
So I went to this hot spring.

00:27:28.320 --> 00:27:29.510
I'm not gonna name the names

00:27:29.510 --> 00:27:31.070
because I can't remember all the names

00:27:31.070 --> 00:27:33.310
and also the whole problem is

00:27:33.310 --> 00:27:35.070
that too many people know about 'em.

00:27:35.070 --> 00:27:37.385
So I'm not gonna talk about where I was.

00:27:37.385 --> 00:27:38.970
Fine, tell me later.

00:27:38.970 --> 00:27:42.483
So I go to this hot
spring that is, you know,

00:27:43.360 --> 00:27:46.731
north of the Alabama Hills in California,

00:27:46.731 --> 00:27:48.990
I know it's confusing,
but you can hang on.

00:27:48.990 --> 00:27:52.400
And it's in the middle of a giant, like

00:27:53.530 --> 00:27:56.500
there's just so many just open valleys

00:27:56.500 --> 00:27:58.460
that are just filled with grazing cattle.

00:27:58.460 --> 00:28:00.550
Like, it's just throughout
all of California

00:28:00.550 --> 00:28:02.420
and all of Nevada it's
just a bunch of cattle

00:28:02.420 --> 00:28:05.800
grazing in the middle of
nowhere, is what it seems like.

00:28:05.800 --> 00:28:08.250
Way more cattle than people
as far as I can tell.

00:28:09.520 --> 00:28:10.353
And there's a hot spring

00:28:10.353 --> 00:28:11.920
out in the middle of this cow field,

00:28:11.920 --> 00:28:15.330
like there's like a
walkway that they've built

00:28:15.330 --> 00:28:17.570
over the cow pasture to kind of keep you

00:28:17.570 --> 00:28:20.970
from like walking
through cow crap and mud.

00:28:20.970 --> 00:28:23.650
And then it just comes to this spring

00:28:23.650 --> 00:28:24.500
in the middle of the field.

00:28:24.500 --> 00:28:27.950
And there's literally like
cow shit around the spring

00:28:27.950 --> 00:28:31.030
and also cows like looking
at you as you're bathing.

00:28:31.030 --> 00:28:32.550
Well, did they get in it?

00:28:32.550 --> 00:28:34.490
The cows don't, it's too hot for the cows.

00:28:34.490 --> 00:28:36.440
So there's two springs.

00:28:36.440 --> 00:28:38.910
One is the one that obviously is preferred

00:28:38.910 --> 00:28:42.560
because there's like nine people in it.

00:28:42.560 --> 00:28:44.010
And there's no room for another person.

00:28:44.010 --> 00:28:45.780
I mean, maybe I could get
awkward and get in there

00:28:45.780 --> 00:28:47.040
but again, I'm thinking about COVID

00:28:47.040 --> 00:28:48.324
and nobody's got a mask on.

00:28:48.324 --> 00:28:49.293
It's dirt, this is a natural spring.

00:28:49.293 --> 00:28:50.830
This is natural.

00:28:50.830 --> 00:28:51.663
'Cause most of the ones

00:28:51.663 --> 00:28:54.647
that we've both experienced
before now have been--

00:28:55.580 --> 00:28:57.350
Like a tub.
Cement lined.

00:28:57.350 --> 00:29:02.350
Well, they'll redirect the
water into a tub or a pool.

00:29:02.360 --> 00:29:04.430
But this was natural.

00:29:04.430 --> 00:29:07.290
But I see that there's like
another thing down there

00:29:07.290 --> 00:29:08.380
that's even more,

00:29:08.380 --> 00:29:10.290
I think it actually the
one that everybody was in,

00:29:10.290 --> 00:29:12.320
they may have reinforced it with rocks,

00:29:12.320 --> 00:29:13.370
but the other one is just like,

00:29:13.370 --> 00:29:15.100
the cow pasture just kind of opens up

00:29:15.100 --> 00:29:18.190
and it's like a foot and a half of water,

00:29:18.190 --> 00:29:20.190
but it's like 20 feet across.

00:29:20.190 --> 00:29:23.100
And there was just one
woman laying in there

00:29:23.100 --> 00:29:24.630
and it was so shallow that like,

00:29:24.630 --> 00:29:26.520
she's kind of laying against the side

00:29:26.520 --> 00:29:28.900
and like most of her
body is out of the water,

00:29:28.900 --> 00:29:29.980
but she's still in the water.

00:29:29.980 --> 00:29:31.250
I'm like, well, I'm gonna do what she does

00:29:31.250 --> 00:29:32.423
and I was like, hey.

00:29:34.530 --> 00:29:35.540
Was she naked?

00:29:35.540 --> 00:29:38.140
No, nobody's naked, she
was fully bathing suited.

00:29:38.140 --> 00:29:38.973
Okay.

00:29:38.973 --> 00:29:43.610
And I sit down and I'm
like, this is pretty nice.

00:29:46.310 --> 00:29:49.190
Like I'm sitting in
this 18 inches of water,

00:29:49.190 --> 00:29:50.820
you know, my legs are under the water

00:29:50.820 --> 00:29:52.680
and maybe up to my belly button

00:29:52.680 --> 00:29:54.080
and then the rest of me is out

00:29:54.080 --> 00:29:55.500
and then I'm kind of
like laying up against

00:29:55.500 --> 00:30:00.030
the side of the thing on the
grass and it's pretty hot.

00:30:00.030 --> 00:30:02.153
And it's also pretty
hot inside the spring,

00:30:02.153 --> 00:30:04.160
it's hot outside and inside.

00:30:04.160 --> 00:30:05.410
But I'm like, this is cool,

00:30:05.410 --> 00:30:07.810
this is not me connecting with anything,

00:30:07.810 --> 00:30:12.020
this is me thinking about
this woman that's across,

00:30:12.020 --> 00:30:13.230
you know what I'm saying?

00:30:13.230 --> 00:30:16.090
Like, not being able to
disconnect from the reality

00:30:16.090 --> 00:30:18.893
that there's another person here and like,

00:30:20.380 --> 00:30:21.870
am I sitting in a weird way?

00:30:21.870 --> 00:30:24.550
You know, like just being self
conscious in general, right?

00:30:24.550 --> 00:30:25.383
Yeah.

00:30:25.383 --> 00:30:26.330
But also thinking about how

00:30:26.330 --> 00:30:28.450
these people over in this
other hot tub are really loud

00:30:28.450 --> 00:30:30.360
and like laughing and
it's kind of boisterous.

00:30:30.360 --> 00:30:33.200
And then all of a sudden
like, oh crap, here they come.

00:30:33.200 --> 00:30:35.840
Three or four families
come over the horizon

00:30:35.840 --> 00:30:40.770
and they descend on
this spring that I'm in.

00:30:40.770 --> 00:30:43.940
And like there's kids
saying like, mom, it stinks!

00:30:43.940 --> 00:30:46.344
Like one kid's like going like, , gagging

00:30:46.344 --> 00:30:48.610
because of the sulfur, yeah.

00:30:48.610 --> 00:30:51.900
And then everybody's like
it's pretty hot, is it hot?

00:30:51.900 --> 00:30:54.040
How hot is it, is it
clean, it's pretty clean!

00:30:54.040 --> 00:30:56.746
Like, this is not enjoyable.

00:30:56.746 --> 00:30:59.720
And they all kind of just
sort of descend on this area

00:30:59.720 --> 00:31:01.010
and then I can just kind of get up.

00:31:01.010 --> 00:31:03.009
I'm like, I don't want this.

00:31:03.009 --> 00:31:03.842
Yeah.

00:31:03.842 --> 00:31:04.730
I get up and I begin to walk over to--

00:31:04.730 --> 00:31:06.960
Funny thing is, they
didn't want it either.

00:31:06.960 --> 00:31:09.850
Yeah, but just like water, you
present me with an obstacle

00:31:09.850 --> 00:31:11.910
and instead of trying to go through it

00:31:11.910 --> 00:31:14.020
I just go around it.

00:31:14.020 --> 00:31:16.440
and I go over to the other,

00:31:16.440 --> 00:31:17.477
I start walking towards the hot spring

00:31:17.477 --> 00:31:21.253
and all those other people
are, turns out, leaving.

00:31:22.270 --> 00:31:24.967
And there's just one dude in there

00:31:24.967 --> 00:31:27.480
and I just go and get into the spring.

00:31:27.480 --> 00:31:29.080
First of all, it's like three feet deep,

00:31:29.080 --> 00:31:30.530
it's deep enough to sit down.

00:31:31.650 --> 00:31:35.163
But he's, the guy says, what's up, duder.

00:31:36.690 --> 00:31:38.580
What's up, duder.
Duder.

00:31:39.720 --> 00:31:42.060
Yeah, you add the R on the end of dude

00:31:42.060 --> 00:31:44.560
and you're not conversation material.

00:31:44.560 --> 00:31:47.650
And then the woman who was
on the other side walks over

00:31:47.650 --> 00:31:48.933
and he's like, hey babe.

00:31:50.120 --> 00:31:52.770
And then I'm like, oh, they're a couple.

00:31:52.770 --> 00:31:53.980
They're a couple.
They're a married couple.

00:31:53.980 --> 00:31:55.460
And boy, let me tell ya,

00:31:55.460 --> 00:31:57.640
I know everything there
is to know about them

00:31:57.640 --> 00:31:59.880
because he would not stop talking.

00:31:59.880 --> 00:32:01.750
He was one of those guys who

00:32:01.750 --> 00:32:04.610
is just constantly saying things,

00:32:04.610 --> 00:32:09.170
but not necessarily anything
that you need to hear.

00:32:09.170 --> 00:32:10.480
Oh no.

00:32:10.480 --> 00:32:11.700
Talking a lot and saying nothing,

00:32:11.700 --> 00:32:13.413
you know what I'm saying?

00:32:13.413 --> 00:32:15.260
Which is why she was all alone over there

00:32:15.260 --> 00:32:17.480
in two inches of water.

00:32:17.480 --> 00:32:19.980
Nice guys, making conversation

00:32:19.980 --> 00:32:23.510
but not the vibe that I'm after, you know?

00:32:23.510 --> 00:32:25.220
And I'm not talkative in those situations.

00:32:25.220 --> 00:32:27.550
I'm more like, right, yeah, uh huh, yeah.

00:32:27.550 --> 00:32:30.030
You were givin' all
the signals to shut up.

00:32:30.030 --> 00:32:32.100
But I knew it was pointless.

00:32:32.100 --> 00:32:34.800
So I was like, Oh, and then eventually

00:32:34.800 --> 00:32:36.700
they were like, duder, we gotta leave?

00:32:37.550 --> 00:32:38.950
They had to go on.

00:32:38.950 --> 00:32:41.660
And I did get some time to myself in this,

00:32:41.660 --> 00:32:43.810
but again, you know, there's
like a family over there

00:32:43.810 --> 00:32:47.580
and I was just like, isn't
what I wanted it to be,

00:32:47.580 --> 00:32:51.010
but I need to just embrace,
I need to embrace what it is.

00:32:51.010 --> 00:32:52.890
But then I was like, okay,
all right, let's leave.

00:32:52.890 --> 00:32:54.723
Let's go to the next place.

00:32:56.452 --> 00:32:58.750
And I was like, I'm just
gonna kind of just go

00:32:58.750 --> 00:33:02.930
and just see where I feel
led to stop and camp.

00:33:02.930 --> 00:33:05.570
Because with this rig I can
basically just go off and go.

00:33:05.570 --> 00:33:07.060
There's all these dirt roads

00:33:07.060 --> 00:33:08.930
off the side of the road, the whole way.

00:33:08.930 --> 00:33:12.040
And it's all public land,
you can just go find a spot.

00:33:12.040 --> 00:33:15.690
So I drive off, I drive up into,

00:33:15.690 --> 00:33:17.740
I get to a place I'm like,
this is a cool like forest.

00:33:17.740 --> 00:33:20.123
This isn't a desert,
this is more like forest.

00:33:21.230 --> 00:33:23.850
I like forest, I'll drive into forest.

00:33:23.850 --> 00:33:24.683
Would you?

00:33:24.683 --> 00:33:27.010
So I drove into the forest

00:33:27.010 --> 00:33:29.290
and started seeing like campsites,

00:33:29.290 --> 00:33:31.200
the road started getting
rougher and I was like,

00:33:31.200 --> 00:33:32.100
I would not be able to do this

00:33:32.100 --> 00:33:33.290
without a four wheel drive vehicle.

00:33:33.290 --> 00:33:35.480
Drove around, found a couple of spots.

00:33:35.480 --> 00:33:38.220
And then eventually kind
of settle in a place.

