00:44:42.180 --> 00:44:44.642
this star light was
incredible, there was no moon.

00:44:44.642 --> 00:44:45.475
There was very little moon.

00:44:45.475 --> 00:44:47.700
I think the beginning of
the trip was the new moon.

00:44:47.700 --> 00:44:50.993
It was up during the day,
but then it set before night.

00:44:52.410 --> 00:44:54.710
So no moonlight so when it gets dark

00:44:54.710 --> 00:44:57.740
you've got your little lantern
there and your headlamp,

00:44:57.740 --> 00:44:59.110
you can't see anything and it's just

00:44:59.110 --> 00:45:01.503
every direction is just pitch black.

00:45:03.282 --> 00:45:05.140
And what could be in that dark

00:45:05.140 --> 00:45:06.710
really started to kind of freak me out.

00:45:06.710 --> 00:45:08.410
And I told the story a long time ago

00:45:08.410 --> 00:45:11.700
about the time I went down
to like burn some stuff

00:45:13.800 --> 00:45:16.343
when I was working for my father in law.

00:45:16.343 --> 00:45:17.520
In the woods, yeah.

00:45:17.520 --> 00:45:19.150
Yeah, and we were like burning,

00:45:19.150 --> 00:45:22.560
it was back when they had to
keep everybody's, you know.

00:45:22.560 --> 00:45:23.773
Medical records.

00:45:23.773 --> 00:45:28.260
You had to keep everybody's
records as paper.

00:45:28.260 --> 00:45:31.510
And then I think it was just
like old patients who had left

00:45:31.510 --> 00:45:34.080
or had died or whatever
and so you could shred 'em

00:45:34.080 --> 00:45:35.420
or you could just burn them or whatever.

00:45:35.420 --> 00:45:37.770
I don't know exactly how
it works 20 years ago.

00:45:38.930 --> 00:45:42.303
But I would be out there burning and then,

00:45:45.201 --> 00:45:46.870
and I had recently watched "The Ring"

00:45:46.870 --> 00:45:49.300
and so I think that the
little girl from "The Ring"

00:45:49.300 --> 00:45:50.900
is gonna like come up out of nowhere

00:45:50.900 --> 00:45:53.070
and I started getting
this image in my mind.

00:45:53.070 --> 00:45:54.640
Well, one of the reasons

00:45:54.640 --> 00:45:57.353
that in The Lost Causes
of Bleak Creek that the,

00:45:59.970 --> 00:46:01.230
not villain, if you will,

00:46:01.230 --> 00:46:04.220
but like one of the scary things in there

00:46:04.220 --> 00:46:09.220
is, spoiler alert, the little girl, Ruby.

00:46:09.998 --> 00:46:13.190
'Cause the idea of a little
girl that's sorta got something

00:46:13.190 --> 00:46:14.800
demonic inside her is the thing

00:46:14.800 --> 00:46:17.040
that is one of the most
scary things for me

00:46:17.040 --> 00:46:20.397
and when I get out there alone.

00:46:20.397 --> 00:46:21.710
You think about her?

00:46:21.710 --> 00:46:23.940
I begin think that like,
I'm gonna turn around

00:46:23.940 --> 00:46:26.640
and there's gonna be like
a little possessed girl

00:46:26.640 --> 00:46:28.870
like on all fours turned over backwards

00:46:28.870 --> 00:46:31.960
like crab crawling at me, like.

00:46:31.960 --> 00:46:33.800
And then the moment I
think about it I'm like,

00:46:33.800 --> 00:46:36.280
you're an idiot, you're a 42 year old man

00:46:36.280 --> 00:46:39.690
out here scared of the dark
and you're thinking about

00:46:39.690 --> 00:46:41.350
things you've seen in horror movies.

00:46:41.350 --> 00:46:42.393
Yeah, yeah.

00:46:42.393 --> 00:46:44.000
And then I like laugh
it off and then I like,

00:46:44.000 --> 00:46:46.733
you know, start like making
my dinner and then I'm like.

00:46:47.780 --> 00:46:48.613
Looking over your shoulder!

00:46:48.613 --> 00:46:50.200
I turn around, yeah, I
hear something and like,

00:46:50.200 --> 00:46:51.603
is she here now?

00:46:52.560 --> 00:46:53.950
Wow.

00:46:53.950 --> 00:46:56.450
And, and then I'm like, man, this is,

00:46:56.450 --> 00:46:59.190
if I knew that there was
somebody 100 yards away,

00:46:59.190 --> 00:47:00.470
I wouldn't be thinking about this.

00:47:00.470 --> 00:47:03.290
Like just the proximity to another person.

00:47:03.290 --> 00:47:05.240
And I was like, isn't that,

00:47:05.240 --> 00:47:06.640
I was like, what does that say about me?

00:47:06.640 --> 00:47:08.870
That I want to be isolated,

00:47:08.870 --> 00:47:11.480
but I want to know where other people are.

00:47:11.480 --> 00:47:13.430
And when I don't know how
far I am from other people

00:47:13.430 --> 00:47:15.330
I began to imagine that
there's possessed people

00:47:15.330 --> 00:47:16.560
who are close.

00:47:16.560 --> 00:47:19.070
It's like, what's going on with me?

00:47:19.070 --> 00:47:22.050
So eventually after it gets too dark

00:47:22.050 --> 00:47:26.040
I get up in my tent and I go to sleep.

00:47:26.040 --> 00:47:29.988
But then that morning I woke
up and again, same thing,

00:47:29.988 --> 00:47:33.690
once the morning comes the
little girl can't come.

00:47:33.690 --> 00:47:34.580
It's how physics works.

00:47:34.580 --> 00:47:36.333
She hates it, she's down in the well.

00:47:36.333 --> 00:47:38.410
She's all, I cannot
come out into the light.

00:47:38.410 --> 00:47:39.960
So I drove, so I had a nice morning,

00:47:39.960 --> 00:47:41.920
I drove down, I drove to a cave

00:47:41.920 --> 00:47:44.750
that had pictographs from
thousands of years ago.

00:47:44.750 --> 00:47:45.583
Wow.

00:47:45.583 --> 00:47:47.270
And it also had metal bars

00:47:47.270 --> 00:47:49.240
that they had put on the
full face of the cave.

00:47:49.240 --> 00:47:50.800
So you can't get in
there and screw 'em up.

00:47:50.800 --> 00:47:51.633
'Cause people.

00:47:51.633 --> 00:47:52.540
Why people gotta be like that?

00:47:52.540 --> 00:47:55.110
Why can't you just not
screw with the pictographs?

00:47:55.110 --> 00:47:57.850
Why you gotta put metal
gates on the front of it?

00:47:57.850 --> 00:48:00.430
Because even the most well meaning people

00:48:01.542 --> 00:48:02.540
are gonna touch it.

00:48:02.540 --> 00:48:04.750
They're gonna just reach
out and touch it and rub it

00:48:04.750 --> 00:48:08.310
before they've thought about
if they should do that or not.

00:48:08.310 --> 00:48:11.222
You rub a pictograph
for thousands of years,

00:48:11.222 --> 00:48:12.610
it's just a rock.

00:48:12.610 --> 00:48:15.810
Yeah, but it was pretty cool,
took some pictures of that.

00:48:15.810 --> 00:48:16.790
Write that in your journal.

00:48:16.790 --> 00:48:19.833
Toquima Cave, I think it's called.

00:48:19.833 --> 00:48:20.666
That sounds right.

00:48:20.666 --> 00:48:22.150
I mean, I took pictures and I was like,

00:48:22.150 --> 00:48:24.060
man, why were they trying to say.

00:48:24.060 --> 00:48:26.170
It looks like just circles and lines.

00:48:26.170 --> 00:48:28.070
Maybe they were just like,
let's draw circles and lines.

00:48:28.070 --> 00:48:29.223
Yeah.
Probably.

00:48:30.210 --> 00:48:31.640
Or maybe this is like representative

00:48:31.640 --> 00:48:33.590
of some sort of contact
they made with aliens,

00:48:33.590 --> 00:48:34.980
which I'm gonna try to do tonight.

00:48:34.980 --> 00:48:36.400
I mean, it could have
been a football coach,

00:48:36.400 --> 00:48:38.850
like doing the offensive play.

00:48:38.850 --> 00:48:40.600
Ancient football.

00:48:40.600 --> 00:48:43.720
So then, again I've got, the cool thing is

00:48:43.720 --> 00:48:48.250
is I've got this, I've
got my app, the Gaia app

00:48:48.250 --> 00:48:50.920
and I've pre downloaded
all the maps for this area,

00:48:50.920 --> 00:48:52.610
for all the places I'm gonna go.

00:48:52.610 --> 00:48:54.350
And it's got layers
that you could turn on.

00:48:54.350 --> 00:48:56.010
Like, this is a US forest service,

00:48:56.010 --> 00:48:58.170
this is the topography, whatever.

00:48:58.170 --> 00:48:59.003
Possessed girl layer.

00:48:59.003 --> 00:49:03.040
This is your cell phone service coverage.

00:49:03.040 --> 00:49:04.371
Like you can see

00:49:04.371 --> 00:49:05.960
where you're gonna get into
cell phone service by carrier.

00:49:05.960 --> 00:49:06.943
I needed that.

00:49:08.620 --> 00:49:11.110
So, super easy, and then you can download

00:49:11.110 --> 00:49:12.350
all the maps at anytime, anyway.

00:49:12.350 --> 00:49:14.130
So I saw that there was a ghost town

00:49:14.130 --> 00:49:15.980
down, like an hour down.

00:49:15.980 --> 00:49:17.210
I mean, it's only like 15 miles away

00:49:17.210 --> 00:49:18.590
but because of the crazy roads and stuff,

00:49:18.590 --> 00:49:20.250
it would take me an hour to get there,

00:49:20.250 --> 00:49:22.280
but a ghost town that had a hot spring.

00:49:22.280 --> 00:49:23.332
Oh.

00:49:23.332 --> 00:49:25.080
And I'm like, okay, all
right, this sounds fine.

00:49:25.080 --> 00:49:27.001
Double cool.

00:49:27.001 --> 00:49:30.577
So there's this part of
Nevada near that Toquima Cave.

00:49:30.577 --> 00:49:32.410
I know where duder's going now.

00:49:32.410 --> 00:49:35.750
There's these sort of the hills

00:49:35.750 --> 00:49:37.590
that have all the trees on them

00:49:37.590 --> 00:49:39.970
that are like conifer trees of some kind,

00:49:39.970 --> 00:49:42.395
like, you know, some kind of pine tree.

00:49:42.395 --> 00:49:43.630
And it's really awesome

00:49:43.630 --> 00:49:46.860
because they're like not
super thick and they just have

00:49:46.860 --> 00:49:49.200
this sort of like
fairytale quality to them.

00:49:49.200 --> 00:49:50.420
But then when you get to the base of them

00:49:50.420 --> 00:49:55.219
it just turns into just field,
and there's usually cattle

00:49:55.219 --> 00:49:57.860
and the people have made
ranches out of all this stuff.

00:49:57.860 --> 00:49:59.420
But dirt roads would just go forever.

00:49:59.420 --> 00:50:01.430
And so I go down this dirt road

00:50:01.430 --> 00:50:06.260
and I get to a place called
Pots Ranch, I think it was.

00:50:06.260 --> 00:50:11.260
And it's just an old house, an
old farmhouse and like a barn

00:50:11.570 --> 00:50:12.880
that looked like they'd been abandoned

00:50:12.880 --> 00:50:14.700
for many, many generations.

00:50:14.700 --> 00:50:17.200
That's cool.
Or many, many decades.

00:50:17.200 --> 00:50:18.720
But probably been around since like,

00:50:18.720 --> 00:50:20.603
you know, 1800s or whatever.

00:50:20.603 --> 00:50:21.436
Could you walk through it?

00:50:21.436 --> 00:50:25.358
Yeah, I took some pictures
and walked in a little bit,

00:50:25.358 --> 00:50:27.610
but it was like the
floor was falling apart.

00:50:27.610 --> 00:50:29.520
I'll share some of those pictures.

00:50:29.520 --> 00:50:31.840
I bet you weren't thinking,
I'm gonna camp here tonight.

00:50:31.840 --> 00:50:33.980
No, 'cause I wasn't far enough,
I hadn't moved far enough.

00:50:33.980 --> 00:50:34.870
I needed to keep moving

00:50:34.870 --> 00:50:36.779
in order to kind of get back to a place

00:50:36.779 --> 00:50:38.170
where I could be close to
home by the end of the trip.

00:50:38.170 --> 00:50:39.240
And this is, and I'm almost at

00:50:39.240 --> 00:50:40.570
my furthest point away from home,

00:50:40.570 --> 00:50:42.983
I'm kind of in the middle
of Nevada at this point.

00:50:44.470 --> 00:50:47.830
But I am like, okay, but
there's a hot spring here.

00:50:47.830 --> 00:50:48.870
Here's where it's at on the map

00:50:48.870 --> 00:50:50.060
and I kind of went past the house

00:50:50.060 --> 00:50:52.508
and went down this dirt
road and I get down

00:50:52.508 --> 00:50:54.050
and I'm like, Oh yes,
this is gonna be awesome,

00:50:54.050 --> 00:50:56.270
there's nobody around for miles.

00:50:56.270 --> 00:50:58.469
And I get down there,
and I'd seen a picture

00:50:58.469 --> 00:51:00.120
of the hot spring on the internet

00:51:00.120 --> 00:51:02.600
'cause I had service for a second.

00:51:02.600 --> 00:51:04.960
And it was the same deal,
it was like, there's a creek

00:51:04.960 --> 00:51:09.003
and they they've run the Creek
through a pipe into a tub.

00:51:09.870 --> 00:51:11.910
I get down there, the tub's gone.

00:51:11.910 --> 00:51:13.927
The pipe literally just is there

00:51:13.927 --> 00:51:16.290
and just goes onto the ground.

00:51:16.290 --> 00:51:19.120
They've removed the tub.
The tub, a ghost.

00:51:19.120 --> 00:51:20.670
There's just a little pool there

00:51:20.670 --> 00:51:22.250
that they've kind of dammed up

00:51:22.250 --> 00:51:24.040
that's about six inches deep.

00:51:24.040 --> 00:51:26.640
So I just like sat there
and stuck my feet in there.

00:51:27.936 --> 00:51:29.453
Like a poor little boy.

00:51:30.461 --> 00:51:32.597
It felt great, but I was like,

00:51:32.597 --> 00:51:34.680
how would this be if the tub was here?

00:51:34.680 --> 00:51:36.470
why you gotta take the tub!

00:51:36.470 --> 00:51:37.603
You should've brought your own tub.

00:51:37.603 --> 00:51:38.690
Next time, man.

00:51:38.690 --> 00:51:39.523
I thought about it, I was like,

00:51:39.523 --> 00:51:42.713
could you have a baby
pool that you inflate?

00:51:43.680 --> 00:51:45.870
Like a mobile hot tub?

00:51:45.870 --> 00:51:47.520
It's gotta be like made a Kevlar.

00:51:49.300 --> 00:51:51.240
They make those for like
really rambunctious children.

00:51:51.240 --> 00:51:52.660
I did have my first shower there.

00:51:52.660 --> 00:51:54.650
I had been in a couple of hot tubs,

00:51:54.650 --> 00:51:56.710
but I had my solar shower,

00:51:56.710 --> 00:51:58.460
like heated the water up with the sun.

00:51:58.460 --> 00:52:02.450
You talking about a SAC with
a tube coming out of it?

00:52:02.450 --> 00:52:05.050
Yeah, basically, solar showers
sounds more sophisticated.

00:52:05.050 --> 00:52:09.223
I shower myself, I use
soap, I wash my parts.

00:52:10.090 --> 00:52:11.870
It feels good, doesn't it?
It feels great.

00:52:11.870 --> 00:52:13.840
If you haven't washed
your parts in a few days.

00:52:13.840 --> 00:52:15.630
You gotta wash your parts.

00:52:15.630 --> 00:52:19.660
And I had a, I was naked
in the middle of a field.

00:52:19.660 --> 00:52:20.630
Yeah, that's nice.

00:52:20.630 --> 00:52:22.560
With mountains on both sides

00:52:22.560 --> 00:52:26.400
and just valley as far
as I could see both ways.

00:52:26.400 --> 00:52:28.640
And I was like, this is pretty awesome.

00:52:28.640 --> 00:52:30.030
I mean, I wish that the tub was here,

00:52:30.030 --> 00:52:31.750
but this is pretty awesome.

00:52:31.750 --> 00:52:33.280
And I began, it's funny,

00:52:33.280 --> 00:52:35.880
the moment that my whole
perspective began to shift

00:52:35.880 --> 00:52:38.930
from like, are you
learning your lesson yet?

00:52:38.930 --> 00:52:41.020
You come with these expectations.

00:52:41.020 --> 00:52:43.150
Drop your expectations, man.

00:52:43.150 --> 00:52:44.000
That's the whole point of this,

00:52:44.000 --> 00:52:45.820
you gotta drop the expectations.

00:52:45.820 --> 00:52:48.415
The tub may not be
there, it doesn't matter.

00:52:48.415 --> 00:52:50.600
Take a shower, wash your parts.

00:52:50.600 --> 00:52:52.545
It's not something you
can just reflect on.

00:52:52.545 --> 00:52:57.545
The way you say it it
does seem like it's like,

00:52:58.250 --> 00:53:00.950
I should've known this, I
should've just realized this.

00:53:00.950 --> 00:53:02.721
You have to experience it.

00:53:02.721 --> 00:53:03.554
But actually, I think that,

00:53:03.554 --> 00:53:05.110
that's the thing that I'm learning

00:53:05.110 --> 00:53:07.493
and what I was saying in
the last one too, was like,

00:53:08.440 --> 00:53:09.990
it made sense when people talked about

00:53:09.990 --> 00:53:11.050
connecting with nature

00:53:11.050 --> 00:53:13.130
or connecting with themselves in nature,

00:53:13.130 --> 00:53:14.510
connecting with God in nature,

00:53:14.510 --> 00:53:19.510
you know, whatever your
intention is, it makes sense.

00:53:21.370 --> 00:53:24.020
But it's different to
actually experience it.

00:53:24.020 --> 00:53:26.590
And I think, I guess that's
true for just lessons in life

00:53:26.590 --> 00:53:30.060
is that like, the
strongest lessons you learn

00:53:30.060 --> 00:53:32.360
are the ones that you experience.

00:53:32.360 --> 00:53:33.940
And which is comforting as a parent,

00:53:33.940 --> 00:53:36.850
and you to think about it
in terms of your kids too,

00:53:36.850 --> 00:53:38.460
that like you can't protect
them from everything.

00:53:38.460 --> 00:53:40.990
And it's like, that's
the most powerful way

00:53:40.990 --> 00:53:45.990
to change who you are as a
person is to go through things.

00:53:46.410 --> 00:53:51.410
And so, but maybe I'm hearing
too much in what you're saying

00:53:52.160 --> 00:53:54.100
that it almost feels like,

00:53:54.100 --> 00:53:56.670
I had to go through this
in order to learn it.

00:53:56.670 --> 00:53:58.350
But it's like, I went through this

00:53:58.350 --> 00:53:59.887
and I was able to learn it.

00:53:59.887 --> 00:54:01.500
That's very much what was happening.

00:54:01.500 --> 00:54:04.150
I had my therapy appointment last night

00:54:04.150 --> 00:54:05.850
to kind of follow up from my trip.

00:54:07.020 --> 00:54:09.860
And of course I ended
up sharing everything

00:54:09.860 --> 00:54:11.720
that happened to me internally.

00:54:11.720 --> 00:54:14.000
Which even that morning
when I'm taking my shower

00:54:14.000 --> 00:54:15.870
and sitting next to this
hot creek and stuff,

00:54:15.870 --> 00:54:18.850
there was a lot of things that
I was just sort of realizing

00:54:18.850 --> 00:54:21.950
and more than just the surface level of,

00:54:21.950 --> 00:54:24.830
this trip represents my
life in a lot of ways

00:54:24.830 --> 00:54:27.150
and the way that I live
and the things that I seek.

00:54:27.150 --> 00:54:28.810
It's not just something
you can write down.

00:54:28.810 --> 00:54:30.860
It's not something that
just happens in your brain,

00:54:30.860 --> 00:54:33.060
is what I think we're saying.

00:54:33.060 --> 00:54:36.380
Yeah, and that was a big,

00:54:36.380 --> 00:54:38.420
I'll share a little bit of
that because I think that was

00:54:38.420 --> 00:54:41.750
one of the biggest realizations
on the trip is that I,

00:54:41.750 --> 00:54:44.410
you know, I've talked
about how I live in my mind

00:54:44.410 --> 00:54:46.270
and I'm a victim of my thoughts,

00:54:46.270 --> 00:54:47.630
which is a very normal thing,

00:54:47.630 --> 00:54:52.630
but that, you know, heart
knowledge, experiencing things,

00:54:53.004 --> 00:54:57.350
something just being true because it is,

00:54:57.350 --> 00:55:00.740
not because you can
rationalize it, explain it,

00:55:00.740 --> 00:55:04.870
analyze it, break it down, and
present it to someone else.

00:55:04.870 --> 00:55:07.480
But something that you can just experience

00:55:07.480 --> 00:55:11.513
and sort of know intrinsically,
like access inside yourself.

00:55:13.470 --> 00:55:17.170
Was something that was just
very visceral and very powerful

00:55:17.170 --> 00:55:19.800
that was continuing to
happen throughout the trip.

00:55:19.800 --> 00:55:23.423
But that day now, you know
I brought the special chair.

00:55:24.300 --> 00:55:26.740
I had intentions to try
to connect with aliens.

00:55:26.740 --> 00:55:27.763
Now, the thing I didn't talk about

00:55:27.763 --> 00:55:30.990
as I had listened to the
30 minute guided meditation

00:55:30.990 --> 00:55:35.665
from Dr. Steven Greer in
the CE5, I think it is app.

00:55:35.665 --> 00:55:38.330
Again, this is from
that Netflix documentary

00:55:38.330 --> 00:55:39.930
which I was fascinated by.

00:55:39.930 --> 00:55:41.237
Again, I don't believe it, I don't--

00:55:41.237 --> 00:55:44.698
"Close Enounters of the Fifth
Kind", is what it's called.

00:55:44.698 --> 00:55:48.024
And basically the fifth kind
representing essentially

00:55:48.024 --> 00:55:49.900
a cosmic connection to aliens.

00:55:49.900 --> 00:55:52.063
Not like just seeing them physically, but.

00:55:52.921 --> 00:55:55.130
Receiving a signal from them.

00:55:55.130 --> 00:55:58.320
It's based on the fact
that if there is something

00:55:58.320 --> 00:56:01.290
to the idea of a collective
consciousness of the universe

00:56:01.290 --> 00:56:04.330
then other intelligent life
out there is a part of that.

00:56:04.330 --> 00:56:07.093
And we can, if we get into that sort of,

00:56:08.179 --> 00:56:11.530
what do you want to call it,
metaphysical, spiritual plane

00:56:11.530 --> 00:56:13.620
there is some way to have a connection.

00:56:13.620 --> 00:56:17.000
Now, again, the rational part of my brain

00:56:17.000 --> 00:56:21.860
which is very dominant in
my mind, in my personality,

00:56:21.860 --> 00:56:24.340
is like, this is almost bullshit, right?

00:56:24.340 --> 00:56:29.340
Like, I would be flummoxed if
this were to be true, right?

00:56:30.090 --> 00:56:32.350
If you really could connect
with aliens in this way.

00:56:32.350 --> 00:56:35.460
But also, why the hell not try?

00:56:35.460 --> 00:56:37.850
Who am I going to hurt if I try?

00:56:37.850 --> 00:56:39.930
And wouldn't it be cool
if it could happen?

00:56:39.930 --> 00:56:41.783
So tonight I'm gonna try.

00:56:43.640 --> 00:56:45.010
'Cause I finally listened to like

00:56:45.010 --> 00:56:48.020
the way he kind of talks
about the meditation process

00:56:48.020 --> 00:56:50.538
and how you're supposed to like basically

00:56:50.538 --> 00:56:54.169
broadcast where you're
at so they'll find you.

00:56:54.169 --> 00:56:55.343
Oh, right.

00:56:56.430 --> 00:56:58.060
But I see this hot spring on the map

00:56:58.060 --> 00:57:00.011
that I'm not too far from and I'm like,

00:57:00.011 --> 00:57:02.890
well, let's go there,
maybe there'll be a tub.

00:57:02.890 --> 00:57:04.420
And it was funny,

00:57:04.420 --> 00:57:07.930
the moment that my expectations
completely evaporated

00:57:07.930 --> 00:57:11.575
is the moment that I found the
best hot spring experience.

00:57:11.575 --> 00:57:13.150
This is the next to last night.

00:57:13.150 --> 00:57:13.983
I stop at a place

00:57:13.983 --> 00:57:15.330
that I'm not going to
tell you where it's at

00:57:15.330 --> 00:57:16.880
because I hope to return to it.

00:57:18.310 --> 00:57:23.310
But I go there and there
is two large families

00:57:25.560 --> 00:57:27.160
with two large trailers

00:57:27.160 --> 00:57:30.407
who are camped right next to the spring.

00:57:30.407 --> 00:57:32.520
And I'm like, ah, crap.

00:57:32.520 --> 00:57:33.950
And they're getting out of the spring

00:57:33.950 --> 00:57:34.783
like they've been bathing,

00:57:34.783 --> 00:57:37.580
like they've taken a
little break or whatever.

00:57:37.580 --> 00:57:39.490
And I kind of just drive up to the spring

00:57:39.490 --> 00:57:41.279
'cause I'm like, I'll just, you know,

00:57:41.279 --> 00:57:42.112
I wasn't planning on camping there.

00:57:42.112 --> 00:57:44.170
I was like, I'm just going
to take a dip, take a soak.

00:57:45.440 --> 00:57:48.330
And I'm like, oh, you
guys got a good spot.

00:57:48.330 --> 00:57:50.640
And he's like, well, we're leaving.

00:57:50.640 --> 00:57:52.936
And you're like, you do that.

00:57:52.936 --> 00:57:54.890
And he was like, and I
just cleaned the hot tub.

00:57:54.890 --> 00:57:58.670
So basically it was a
heart shaped stone tub.

00:57:58.670 --> 00:58:01.110
That the way it works is, again,
they all work the same way,

00:58:01.110 --> 00:58:03.410
you've got the hot water
source from the spring

00:58:03.410 --> 00:58:05.780
that somebody has piped into this thing.

00:58:05.780 --> 00:58:09.270
It fills up and then it kind
of goes out over a spillway

00:58:09.270 --> 00:58:11.120
but there's a plug in the
bottom of a lot of these

00:58:11.120 --> 00:58:13.546
that you can pull, so it'll drain it

00:58:13.546 --> 00:58:14.538
and then you can scrub it.

00:58:14.538 --> 00:58:15.620
And the dude, because they
had been there for a few days,

00:58:15.620 --> 00:58:17.330
antelope hunting, didn't know

00:58:17.330 --> 00:58:18.250
that was something that happened,

00:58:18.250 --> 00:58:20.020
they had two antelopes already.

00:58:20.020 --> 00:58:22.800
Like draped over their
trailers or something?

00:58:22.800 --> 00:58:24.061
Butchered or something?

00:58:24.061 --> 00:58:26.610
I didn't see 'em, he just
said, we've killed two.

00:58:26.610 --> 00:58:27.770
Huh?

00:58:27.770 --> 00:58:29.180
He drained and scrubbed the thing.

00:58:29.180 --> 00:58:30.721
He had drained and scrubbed it

00:58:30.721 --> 00:58:31.670
and he had just filled it up

00:58:31.670 --> 00:58:33.350
and he like showed me how it worked.

00:58:33.350 --> 00:58:34.183
That's cool.

00:58:34.183 --> 00:58:36.310
And then they left and I was like.

00:58:36.310 --> 00:58:37.143
Oh yes.

00:58:37.143 --> 00:58:40.373
Am I about to have a heart
shaped tub all to myself?

00:58:41.500 --> 00:58:43.190
No, I'm not.
Oh.

00:58:43.190 --> 00:58:45.410
Because, now this is like two o'clock,

00:58:45.410 --> 00:58:47.740
I was like, there's no way
no one's gonna show up.

00:58:47.740 --> 00:58:51.430
But I was like, you know
what, I'm gonna stay here,

00:58:51.430 --> 00:58:52.940
I'm gonna camp here.

00:58:52.940 --> 00:58:56.790
So I go ahead and I take my
rooftop tent out, I set it up.

00:58:56.790 --> 00:58:59.450
I'm probably, I don't
know what the etiquette is

00:58:59.450 --> 00:59:02.280
but I'm probably closer to the
hot spring than I should be.

00:59:02.280 --> 00:59:05.197
But I was like, I wanna get in it.

00:59:05.197 --> 00:59:06.770
Not that nobody else was gonna get in it

00:59:06.770 --> 00:59:09.920
but I'm probably 100 feet from
it, so it was pretty close.

00:59:09.920 --> 00:59:14.920
And then up rolls two cars,
two older couples get out

00:59:17.729 --> 00:59:21.053
and they walk up to me and they're like,

00:59:22.870 --> 00:59:25.693
so, is that the spring?

00:59:27.150 --> 00:59:30.760
And then it hits me, I'm like,
they think I'm an authority.

00:59:30.760 --> 00:59:31.710
Yes.

00:59:31.710 --> 00:59:34.780
Because I'm here and because
I'm close to the spring

00:59:34.780 --> 00:59:37.570
they assume that I have some sort of

00:59:37.570 --> 00:59:39.660
intrinsic authority here.

00:59:39.660 --> 00:59:41.020
This is how people's brains work.

00:59:41.020 --> 00:59:42.540
You can use it against them.

00:59:42.540 --> 00:59:44.480
And I was like--
You could say no.

00:59:44.480 --> 00:59:46.160
Yeah, it's just been cleaned.

00:59:46.160 --> 00:59:47.604
Oh no.

00:59:47.604 --> 00:59:49.390
Shoulda said it's dirty.
No, no, no.

00:59:49.390 --> 00:59:50.360
I played it like this.

00:59:50.360 --> 00:59:53.136
I'm gonna be generous with this spring.

00:59:53.136 --> 00:59:54.141
Okay.

00:59:54.141 --> 00:59:54.974
I'm the keeper of the spring,

00:59:54.974 --> 00:59:57.720
That's what these people
believe, I'm gonna be generous.

00:59:57.720 --> 00:59:59.600
And I said, it's just been cleaned,

00:59:59.600 --> 01:00:02.253
it should be warmed up for ya.

01:00:03.180 --> 01:00:04.013
And that, and they're like,

01:00:04.013 --> 01:00:05.570
well, we're just gonna
stay for a little bit.

01:00:05.570 --> 01:00:07.403
I mean, if you don't mind.

01:00:09.578 --> 01:00:10.790
I was like, this is working.

01:00:10.790 --> 01:00:13.310
And I'm like, take all the time you want.

01:00:13.310 --> 01:00:15.390
As if I have any authority at all,

01:00:15.390 --> 01:00:16.240
but I'm just going with it

01:00:16.240 --> 01:00:17.450
'cause they seem to be going with it.

01:00:17.450 --> 01:00:18.410
Okay.

01:00:18.410 --> 01:00:22.020
But you coulda also tell
'em, take 10 minutes.

01:00:22.020 --> 01:00:24.270
No, 'cause if you give somebody a limit

01:00:24.270 --> 01:00:26.670
they naturally want to exceed it.

01:00:26.670 --> 01:00:28.490
Don't give 'em a limit, just be generous.

01:00:28.490 --> 01:00:31.220
Be nice and then they're
like, I'ma give him his space.

01:00:31.220 --> 01:00:33.200
He lives here, look at his beard.

01:00:33.200 --> 01:00:34.300
They were from Ohio.

01:00:35.140 --> 01:00:36.510
I said, you know, Chuck Testa?

01:00:36.510 --> 01:00:38.310
And they were like, oh
yeah, the commercial.

01:00:38.310 --> 01:00:40.283
I was like, I made that commercial.

01:00:41.982 --> 01:00:43.610
Really?

01:00:43.610 --> 01:00:46.090
And boy, that got me some brownie points.

01:00:46.090 --> 01:00:47.170
Seriously?
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:00:47.170 --> 01:00:49.865
They're like, he keeps the
spring and he makes commercials.

01:00:49.865 --> 01:00:51.653
How does he do it all?

01:00:52.730 --> 01:00:54.140
'Cause that is all.

01:00:54.140 --> 01:00:58.140
And so I talked to them for a while.

01:00:58.140 --> 01:00:59.280
You were in the spring?

01:00:59.280 --> 01:01:00.113
No, no, no.

01:01:01.565 --> 01:01:02.398
You were standing over it keeping it.

01:01:02.398 --> 01:01:07.300
I was sitting in my alien
chair next to my truck,

01:01:09.000 --> 01:01:11.230
on this side where you had to walk past me

01:01:11.230 --> 01:01:12.920
to get to the spring.

01:01:12.920 --> 01:01:15.110
That couple ended up leaving,
another couple showed up,

01:01:15.110 --> 01:01:16.270
a younger couple.

01:01:16.270 --> 01:01:17.950
That was the #vanlife couple

01:01:17.950 --> 01:01:19.030
where they were taking the pictures

01:01:19.030 --> 01:01:20.720
that I told you about during your story.

01:01:20.720 --> 01:01:22.330
Yeah, priorities out of whack.

01:01:22.330 --> 01:01:23.640
They ended up being really cool.

01:01:23.640 --> 01:01:26.773
They stayed the night and an
older man stayed the night.

01:01:27.670 --> 01:01:32.670
Now, not really close to me
and the way it ends up working

01:01:33.110 --> 01:01:34.150
is you ended up spending,

01:01:34.150 --> 01:01:35.830
you can spend about 10 or 15 minutes

01:01:35.830 --> 01:01:36.900
in a really hot, hot tub.

01:01:36.900 --> 01:01:40.480
This one, the temperature
of the water was 130 degrees

01:01:40.480 --> 01:01:42.728
coming out of the pipe so
you'd have to like, fill it up

01:01:42.728 --> 01:01:47.728
and then divert it or put
a restrictor in the pipe.

01:01:47.906 --> 01:01:50.483
The engineering behind
these things is pretty cool.

01:01:51.500 --> 01:01:52.510
Put a restrictor in it

01:01:52.510 --> 01:01:54.680
so it's not quite filling up fast enough

01:01:54.680 --> 01:01:55.940
and it slowly losing temperature

01:01:55.940 --> 01:01:57.790
so it probably gets down to like 110.

01:01:59.499 --> 01:02:03.617
And so that night I just got
into the hot tub by myself

01:02:04.750 --> 01:02:07.903
for 20, 30 minutes, looked at the stars.

01:02:08.862 --> 01:02:12.998
Everyone was completely
quiet, they were both gone,

01:02:12.998 --> 01:02:16.730
they were in their van and
in their camper asleep,

01:02:16.730 --> 01:02:19.290
not talking, and I'm just
out there and I'm like,

01:02:19.290 --> 01:02:21.780
man, this is awesome, I get it.

01:02:21.780 --> 01:02:23.750
Like, I'm in this heart shaped hot tub.

01:02:23.750 --> 01:02:25.160
And then it hit me like, oh,

01:02:25.160 --> 01:02:26.730
I need to reach out to the aliens.

01:02:26.730 --> 01:02:27.563
Yeah.

01:02:27.563 --> 01:02:28.890
So I began the meditative process,

01:02:28.890 --> 01:02:30.940
I went through the steps,
which ended with me.

01:02:30.940 --> 01:02:32.853
I'm in the heart shaped hot tub.

01:02:34.050 --> 01:02:35.700
You know the one.

01:02:35.700 --> 01:02:37.270
Oh yeah, 'cause you start.

01:02:37.270 --> 01:02:40.900
You start with a galaxy, you
move to the solar system.

01:02:40.900 --> 01:02:42.740
You funnel your signal down.

01:02:42.740 --> 01:02:45.510
You move to the North America,
you move into America,

01:02:45.510 --> 01:02:48.571
you move to Nevada, bomba,
bomba, bomba down to there.

01:02:48.571 --> 01:02:49.853
And let me tell you.

01:02:51.707 --> 01:02:52.540
That was the moment, right?

01:02:52.540 --> 01:02:53.910
You're like, I'm right here.

01:02:53.910 --> 01:02:55.700
I'm here, I'm like, you can't miss me.

01:02:55.700 --> 01:02:57.063
Heart shaped hot tub.

01:02:59.770 --> 01:03:02.180
And the thing I can see
most closely relate it to

01:03:02.180 --> 01:03:06.360
is the time I tried to speak
in tongues in New York.

01:03:06.360 --> 01:03:08.420
When I was trying to share the gospel

01:03:08.420 --> 01:03:10.560
with the man on the bench
who couldn't speak English.

01:03:10.560 --> 01:03:12.513
And I was like, Lord, this is when,

01:03:12.513 --> 01:03:15.870
this is when I speak
in tongues, this is it.

01:03:15.870 --> 01:03:18.470
And I was like, almost,
going to open my mouth

01:03:18.470 --> 01:03:19.970
and just see what happens, you know.

01:03:19.970 --> 01:03:21.290
Because I was under the understanding

01:03:21.290 --> 01:03:23.310
that speaking in tongues was
sort of angelic purposes,

01:03:23.310 --> 01:03:24.860
not for the edification of the body.

01:03:24.860 --> 01:03:27.530
That's the theological stuff
that no one cares about

01:03:27.530 --> 01:03:30.130
unless you're one of those
people who cares about it.

01:03:30.130 --> 01:03:30.973
Anyway,

01:03:34.140 --> 01:03:36.310
they didn't come, they didn't show up.

01:03:36.310 --> 01:03:37.820
I did it twice.

01:03:37.820 --> 01:03:40.520
And I was so, I was so committed.

01:03:40.520 --> 01:03:42.560
I was like, I am willing to believe this.

01:03:42.560 --> 01:03:46.880
Like, if you just show
up, I am here, I am ready.

01:03:46.880 --> 01:03:48.838
There's also other people
here, but they're sleeping.

01:03:48.838 --> 01:03:50.840
It didn't happen, it didn't happen.

01:03:50.840 --> 01:03:52.300
I'm not saying I'll never try again,

01:03:52.300 --> 01:03:54.410
but I tried so hard that night.

01:03:54.410 --> 01:03:55.710
I can't imagine trying harder,

01:03:55.710 --> 01:03:58.580
but Dr. Steven Greer
suggest doing it in a group.

01:03:58.580 --> 01:04:00.140
They were there.

01:04:00.140 --> 01:04:01.550
You were in the leadership position.

01:04:01.550 --> 01:04:03.470
You have to have
commitment from everybody.

01:04:03.470 --> 01:04:04.410
I think you gotta have buy in,

01:04:04.410 --> 01:04:06.073
that would have been a lot to ask.

01:04:08.795 --> 01:04:11.295
I was gonna say sorry,
but you know what, water.

01:04:12.890 --> 01:04:13.860
I was fine, I was like,

01:04:13.860 --> 01:04:16.530
I wasn't supposed to meet
the aliens on this trip.

01:04:16.530 --> 01:04:18.320
Now I know I'm getting a little bit long,

01:04:18.320 --> 01:04:19.740
there's something significant
I gotta talk about.

01:04:19.740 --> 01:04:20.573
Sure.

01:04:22.390 --> 01:04:23.693
Before the last night,

01:04:26.650 --> 01:04:29.560
I'm like, okay, I gotta
find a place to stop here.

01:04:29.560 --> 01:04:31.870
I was kind of doing the game
that you were doing which is,

01:04:31.870 --> 01:04:33.400
I want to get kind of close to Vegas

01:04:33.400 --> 01:04:35.200
'cause that's gonna be close to home,

01:04:35.200 --> 01:04:36.900
that's like four hours from home.

01:04:36.900 --> 01:04:38.720
But it's hot down there
so where am I gonna go?

01:04:38.720 --> 01:04:41.430
So I stopped about an
hour outside of Vegas,

01:04:41.430 --> 01:04:45.730
north of Vegas in some sort
of higher mountainy areas.

01:04:45.730 --> 01:04:46.920
It's still kind of deserty,

01:04:46.920 --> 01:04:49.923
but about 10 degrees cooler
because of the elevation.

01:04:51.650 --> 01:04:56.400
And as I was looking for my
campsite, I passed by this sign,

01:04:56.400 --> 01:04:58.700
It said, Trilobite Fossil Area.

01:04:58.700 --> 01:04:59.783
Oh.

01:04:59.783 --> 01:05:00.616
And I was like, oh wow.

01:05:00.616 --> 01:05:03.720
I've always wanted to find a
fossil, it's one of my dreams.

01:05:03.720 --> 01:05:06.680
I've never found one, I've
searched the world over.

01:05:06.680 --> 01:05:09.690
Well, I've looked on the
side of the road a few times.

01:05:09.690 --> 01:05:11.740
So I'm like, well, let
me find my campsite first

01:05:11.740 --> 01:05:13.510
and then I'll come back to this area

01:05:13.510 --> 01:05:15.210
because I want to kind of
know where I'm gonna sleep.

01:05:15.210 --> 01:05:17.062
10 minutes down the road I find a spot

01:05:17.062 --> 01:05:20.270
way down in this dirt road
next to this pile of rocks

01:05:20.270 --> 01:05:21.180
where I did end up camping.

01:05:21.180 --> 01:05:24.287
But I go back to the
Trilobite Fossil Area.

01:05:24.287 --> 01:05:27.220
You park your car and
you walk up this path.

01:05:27.220 --> 01:05:30.810
And I like hear a kid's voice
and I hadn't seen another car

01:05:30.810 --> 01:05:32.520
and it felt like I was
in the middle of nowhere

01:05:32.520 --> 01:05:34.000
so I was like, oh, is that the aliens here

01:05:34.000 --> 01:05:35.253
in the form of a child?

01:05:36.400 --> 01:05:37.410
They have kids too.

01:05:37.410 --> 01:05:40.280
It was just a family, a human family.

01:05:40.280 --> 01:05:44.303
And they were getting the fossils.

01:05:44.303 --> 01:05:45.310
There's the shale deposit out there

01:05:45.310 --> 01:05:49.347
where there's this millions,
you know of, trilobites,

01:05:49.347 --> 01:05:51.860
mostly just heads and some full bodies.

01:05:51.860 --> 01:05:53.910
Really?
I didn't see any of 'em yet.

01:05:55.210 --> 01:05:56.680
But like it's this shale,

01:05:56.680 --> 01:05:58.870
it's these very flat
rocks that you pick up.

01:05:58.870 --> 01:06:00.885
And I was like, what do you do, just,

01:06:00.885 --> 01:06:01.718
I start talking to the family,

01:06:01.718 --> 01:06:02.551
I'm like, you just turn rocks over?

01:06:02.551 --> 01:06:04.610
And they're like, well
actually we have a hammer

01:06:04.610 --> 01:06:07.760
and you have to hit the
rocks and they'll split

01:06:07.760 --> 01:06:09.190
and then you see if
there's fossils in there.

01:06:09.190 --> 01:06:12.730
I'm like, ah, I've got an axe
in my truck, I'll go get it.

01:06:12.730 --> 01:06:14.650
It's like, as I said it, I was like,

01:06:14.650 --> 01:06:17.370
I'm going to kill you is what they heard.

01:06:17.370 --> 01:06:18.203
Yes.

01:06:18.203 --> 01:06:20.370
That way you don't run when
I come at you with an axe.

01:06:20.370 --> 01:06:22.524
I'm going to get my axe, family.

01:06:22.524 --> 01:06:26.380
So I turn around, I go
to get my little axe

01:06:26.380 --> 01:06:27.610
and I'm coming back down the path--

01:06:27.610 --> 01:06:28.922
And they're running.

01:06:28.922 --> 01:06:30.320
And no, I get back and
a little boy is crying.

01:06:30.320 --> 01:06:32.190
I'm like, I'm just gonna
use it for the fossils,

01:06:32.190 --> 01:06:34.370
I'm not gonna kill you with it.

01:06:34.370 --> 01:06:37.290
The little boy is crying
because he's locked his keys,

01:06:37.290 --> 01:06:39.680
his parent's keys in their car.

01:06:39.680 --> 01:06:41.510
And they're like, can you help us?

01:06:41.510 --> 01:06:43.100
And I'm like, oh, I don't know,

01:06:43.100 --> 01:06:45.033
I don't really want to
help anyone right now.

01:06:48.226 --> 01:06:49.810
I'm about to get a fossil.

01:06:49.810 --> 01:06:51.510
I mean, I can bust your window.

01:06:51.510 --> 01:06:54.303
It's getting dark, you know,
I'm here for my fossil.

01:06:55.528 --> 01:06:56.940
But I'm like, sure, what happened?

01:06:56.940 --> 01:06:58.560
They're like, he locked
the keys in the car.

01:06:58.560 --> 01:07:00.330
Our campsite where our camper's at

01:07:00.330 --> 01:07:03.190
is just a couple miles down
the road, could you take us?

01:07:03.190 --> 01:07:04.970
I was like, well, I got room for one,

01:07:04.970 --> 01:07:06.800
which sounds like something
a serial killer would say,

01:07:06.800 --> 01:07:10.283
I realize that now, but
I only had room for one.

01:07:10.283 --> 01:07:12.223
Did you have pictures that
you wanted them to vote on?

01:07:12.223 --> 01:07:15.428
Because you are becoming that guy.

01:07:15.428 --> 01:07:16.403
I showed them all my pictures.

01:07:17.324 --> 01:07:18.157
You want to sell them.

01:07:18.157 --> 01:07:23.157
So I took the dad and he
got the key and came back

01:07:23.280 --> 01:07:25.290
and they were like super
appreciative, whatever.

01:07:25.290 --> 01:07:26.650
That was my biggest fear, by the way,

01:07:26.650 --> 01:07:29.450
I only had one key to the sprinter van.

01:07:29.450 --> 01:07:31.410
I think it's smart enough
not to let you lock it

01:07:31.410 --> 01:07:34.227
when the keys are in it, but
I was very anxious about that.

01:07:34.227 --> 01:07:36.177
Yeah, I had an extra for that reason.

01:07:37.880 --> 01:07:41.330
You rescued them.
It was a give and take.

01:07:41.330 --> 01:07:42.860
I saved the day for them,

01:07:42.860 --> 01:07:44.480
they would probably be dead without me.

01:07:44.480 --> 01:07:45.800
Sure.

01:07:45.800 --> 01:07:47.003
But the thing that they provided is--

01:07:47.003 --> 01:07:49.540
They would have killed
the kid out of anger.

01:07:49.540 --> 01:07:50.610
The thing that they provided

01:07:50.610 --> 01:07:53.090
is the method how to get fossils.

01:07:53.090 --> 01:07:54.620
I would have been out
there turning rocks over,

01:07:54.620 --> 01:07:56.610
and let me tell you, I would
have never found anything

01:07:56.610 --> 01:07:57.740
'cause you have to split the rocks

01:07:57.740 --> 01:07:59.740
'cause everyone's turned the rocks over.

01:08:00.680 --> 01:08:02.280
They had found three of these things

01:08:02.280 --> 01:08:04.692
so I was confident that
I was gonna find one.

01:08:04.692 --> 01:08:06.050
But I was like, it's getting dark,

01:08:06.050 --> 01:08:07.720
but I'm going to find
my first ever fossil.

01:08:07.720 --> 01:08:09.840
And then I began to attach

01:08:09.840 --> 01:08:11.980
a lot of expectation to finding a fossil

01:08:11.980 --> 01:08:14.050
and the sun was going down

01:08:14.050 --> 01:08:16.620
and I'm frantically running
around, hitting these rocks.

01:08:16.620 --> 01:08:18.770
And I'm like, I've never
gonna find a fossil!

01:08:18.770 --> 01:08:20.623
I'm not a guy that finds fossils.

01:08:21.870 --> 01:08:22.703
You're moving fast.

01:08:22.703 --> 01:08:24.980
I'm having a little bit of a pity party.

01:08:24.980 --> 01:08:27.340
And then I'm trying other things like,

01:08:27.340 --> 01:08:32.340
maybe if I close my eyes and
walk and just set my hands down

01:08:32.370 --> 01:08:34.330
I will be directed to
where the fossils are.

01:08:34.330 --> 01:08:37.040
You were sincerely thinking that?

01:08:37.040 --> 01:08:38.384
Oh, I did that.

01:08:38.384 --> 01:08:40.130
If you had to watch me,
it would have been like,

01:08:40.130 --> 01:08:43.280
I was just laying back
and then I was like,

01:08:43.280 --> 01:08:45.040
I sense the fossil and I
would go down and touch it

01:08:45.040 --> 01:08:46.060
and try it, and nothing.

01:08:46.060 --> 01:08:47.910
That's like, I mean,

01:08:47.910 --> 01:08:50.960
that's like praying to win
your recreation soccer game.

01:08:50.960 --> 01:08:52.500
Yeah, which I also used to do.

01:08:52.500 --> 01:08:53.924
Yeah, right.

01:08:53.924 --> 01:08:57.366
So then I was like, okay,
well it's getting dark.

01:08:57.366 --> 01:08:59.680
You know what, three more rocks.

01:08:59.680 --> 01:09:00.970
And then I do three and not find one.

01:09:00.970 --> 01:09:03.617
Okay, two more rocks.
Two more.

01:09:03.617 --> 01:09:07.780
I kept doing that strategy
until I finally found one.

01:09:07.780 --> 01:09:09.910
Now, I don't have it with me because,

01:09:09.910 --> 01:09:10.900
the reason I didn't bring it

01:09:10.900 --> 01:09:13.630
is because I'm afraid that
if I showed it to you,

01:09:13.630 --> 01:09:15.270
you'd be like, that's a fossil?

01:09:15.270 --> 01:09:17.580
You know what I'm saying, it's
like, it's not impressive.

01:09:17.580 --> 01:09:20.120
It's the head of a
trilobite, it's really small,

01:09:20.120 --> 01:09:23.010
it's very clearly a fossil
if you really look at it,

01:09:23.010 --> 01:09:24.530
but it's not like,

01:09:24.530 --> 01:09:26.340
this thing ain't going in the Smithsonian,

01:09:26.340 --> 01:09:27.173
you know what I'm saying?

01:09:27.173 --> 01:09:29.530
Like, and all the ones out there are like,

01:09:29.530 --> 01:09:31.960
oh, I can see the outline
of where this thing died,

01:09:31.960 --> 01:09:34.830
but it's not this even a discoloration

01:09:34.830 --> 01:09:38.450
as much as it's an imprint
of this ancient crustacean

01:09:38.450 --> 01:09:39.283
or whatever they are.

01:09:39.283 --> 01:09:40.740
But it's special to you.
It's special to me

01:09:40.740 --> 01:09:43.740
and it's sort of my, what
is the word, talisman?

01:09:43.740 --> 01:09:45.420
Talisman.

01:09:45.420 --> 01:09:46.253
Amulet?

01:09:46.253 --> 01:09:47.908
For my trip, you know.

01:09:47.908 --> 01:09:49.230
Souvenir.

01:09:49.230 --> 01:09:51.560
Souvenir that I kind of hold on to,

01:09:51.560 --> 01:09:54.310
the significant life things that happened

01:09:54.310 --> 01:09:56.880
because I've got this thing to
kind of hold on to, whatever.

01:09:56.880 --> 01:09:58.400
Keep it in your pocket at all times.

01:09:58.400 --> 01:10:01.150
So I found that, then the
last night of camping,

01:10:01.150 --> 01:10:05.513
camping next to a rock
pile, a big rock pile.

01:10:07.810 --> 01:10:10.020
And in the middle of this giant valley

01:10:10.020 --> 01:10:12.190
there's this giant rock pile,

01:10:12.190 --> 01:10:14.090
kind of like the Joshua Tree tight rocks

01:10:14.090 --> 01:10:15.443
but a little bit different.

01:10:16.930 --> 01:10:19.270
And I get there as it's getting dark,

01:10:19.270 --> 01:10:20.420
the sun has already
gone down but I'm like,

01:10:20.420 --> 01:10:22.680
I gotta really set up really quickly.

01:10:22.680 --> 01:10:26.080
And I like to get out
of the car and I'm like,

01:10:26.080 --> 01:10:27.183
whoa, what was that?

01:10:28.800 --> 01:10:29.633
A bat.

01:10:29.633 --> 01:10:30.850
You're afraid of bats.

01:10:30.850 --> 01:10:34.190
I hate bats, and I have
set up next to a bat cave.

01:10:34.190 --> 01:10:35.023
Oh.

01:10:35.900 --> 01:10:36.910
'Cause there's multiple bats

01:10:36.910 --> 01:10:39.683
coming out of the giant rock pile.

01:10:40.660 --> 01:10:42.690
I'm like, I set up next to a bat cave.

01:10:42.690 --> 01:10:43.630
And I'm not Batman.

01:10:43.630 --> 01:10:46.880
You know how they see,
they see by yelling at you.

01:10:46.880 --> 01:10:49.400
And they're attracted to bugs

01:10:49.400 --> 01:10:52.060
and I'm the only source
of light in this valley.

01:10:52.060 --> 01:10:53.570
What do bugs like?

01:10:53.570 --> 01:10:55.570
Lights.
Lights.

01:10:55.570 --> 01:10:57.400
So I'm like, well, I'm gonna see

01:10:57.400 --> 01:10:59.855
if I can do everything in the dark.

01:10:59.855 --> 01:11:02.010
Seriously?
I'm so afraid of bats

01:11:02.914 --> 01:11:05.020
that I was like I'm gonna
cook my dinner in the dark.

01:11:05.020 --> 01:11:06.400
I'm gonna do everything in the dark.

01:11:06.400 --> 01:11:07.580
I kept lights off

01:11:07.580 --> 01:11:09.390
and I was able to do almost
everything in the dark.

01:11:09.390 --> 01:11:12.172
There's a few things you had
to turn the lights on for.

01:11:12.172 --> 01:11:13.650
And at that point there
were moths coming up to me

01:11:13.650 --> 01:11:15.370
and the bats were coming to the moths,

01:11:15.370 --> 01:11:17.126
like, it was a little freaky.

01:11:17.126 --> 01:11:18.853
They didn't attack me.

01:11:21.066 --> 01:11:26.066
But I got in my tent that
night and this is where,

01:11:26.606 --> 01:11:30.110
now I'm finally getting
to these two realizations,

01:11:30.110 --> 01:11:33.230
one related to our conversation

01:11:33.230 --> 01:11:35.020
and another one sort of
a larger scale thing,

01:11:35.020 --> 01:11:36.293
but also kind of related.

01:11:42.253 --> 01:11:43.623
2:30 in the morning.

01:11:45.440 --> 01:11:50.440
I wake up and I'm like,
this doesn't happen to me.

01:11:51.049 --> 01:11:53.020
I wake up in the middle of the night,

01:11:53.020 --> 01:11:56.080
but take a wiz, back to
sleep, whatever, you know?

01:11:56.080 --> 01:11:56.913
Yeah.

01:11:56.913 --> 01:12:01.180
But I can't sleep and then
I could not stop thinking.

01:12:01.180 --> 01:12:03.453
It is very weird because
it was very vivid.

01:12:05.110 --> 01:12:07.930
I could not stop thinking about

01:12:07.930 --> 01:12:10.720
how polarized our country is.

01:12:10.720 --> 01:12:12.703
Like, it was very strange.

01:12:12.703 --> 01:12:16.083
And I think it had something
to do with the fact that,

01:12:17.270 --> 01:12:19.370
you know, I had been thinking
about these wildfires

01:12:19.370 --> 01:12:20.980
the whole time that I'm on this trip

01:12:20.980 --> 01:12:22.580
and I'd been trying to avoid the smoke

01:12:22.580 --> 01:12:25.030
and I knew I was about to
go back into the smoke.

01:12:27.192 --> 01:12:28.880
And I had been on the
internet a little bit.

01:12:28.880 --> 01:12:30.820
Like if I had service,
I was like, checking in.

01:12:30.820 --> 01:12:32.753
You know, there was a
few things happening.

01:12:33.930 --> 01:12:36.140
Like a few emails or whatever
that I like looked at.

01:12:36.140 --> 01:12:37.850
I wasn't completely disconnected,

01:12:37.850 --> 01:12:40.450
I tried not to set some
unrealistic standard for myself,

01:12:40.450 --> 01:12:42.040
but I was like, I'm not gonna
be on the internet very much

01:12:42.040 --> 01:12:44.829
but if I have service,
I might check something.

01:12:44.829 --> 01:12:46.850
And I made the mistake of going
on Twitter a couple of times

01:12:46.850 --> 01:12:51.850
and of course, when you go to Twitter,

01:12:52.100 --> 01:12:53.560
especially Twitter, I mean Facebook,

01:12:53.560 --> 01:12:55.863
which I don't go to 'cause
it's even worse but,

01:12:57.200 --> 01:13:00.520
everyone has an opinion
about these wildfires, right?

01:13:00.520 --> 01:13:03.680
And of course I had been
around the COVID denier guy

01:13:03.680 --> 01:13:05.360
and I was like, I'm just like,

01:13:05.360 --> 01:13:06.330
I'm looking at what people are saying

01:13:06.330 --> 01:13:10.410
about the wildfires
online and it's, you know,

01:13:10.410 --> 01:13:12.699
what our president is
saying about the wildfires

01:13:12.699 --> 01:13:15.320
and there's this narrative that,

01:13:15.320 --> 01:13:20.190
oh, the problem with the West
coast is forest mismanagement.

01:13:20.190 --> 01:13:21.960
Like that's what's
causing these wildfires.

01:13:21.960 --> 01:13:24.460
It's not climate change,
climate change is a hoax.

01:13:25.310 --> 01:13:28.790
And I'm just like, this is
so disheartening, right?

01:13:28.790 --> 01:13:30.340
It's just so disheartening that

01:13:31.186 --> 01:13:34.970
we're dealing with something so tragic,

01:13:34.970 --> 01:13:37.364
that we're just beginning to deal with it.

01:13:37.364 --> 01:13:40.750
It's gonna be just
continued further tragedy

01:13:40.750 --> 01:13:43.070
for the coming decades.

01:13:43.070 --> 01:13:47.400
And it is related to climate
change, but you know what,

01:13:47.400 --> 01:13:51.350
there is also an element
of forest mismanagement

01:13:51.350 --> 01:13:53.090
and that contributes to it, right?

01:13:53.090 --> 01:13:57.355
It's not one or the other.

01:13:57.355 --> 01:14:01.840
It actually, both contribute to it.

01:14:01.840 --> 01:14:05.400
But we're so divided that--
You can't give an inch.

01:14:05.400 --> 01:14:08.540
You have to be on one side or the other.

01:14:08.540 --> 01:14:11.250
And if you believe that
it's forest mismanagement

01:14:11.250 --> 01:14:14.140
you have to believe that
climate change is a hoax

01:14:14.140 --> 01:14:16.850
even though I'm not gonna
give all the arguments

01:14:16.850 --> 01:14:18.360
for climate change, but it's happening

01:14:18.360 --> 01:14:22.330
and it is almost assuredly
caused by humans.

01:14:22.330 --> 01:14:24.020
And if you're on that side of it,

01:14:24.020 --> 01:14:26.440
then you won't listen to anybody
who's got anything to say

01:14:26.440 --> 01:14:28.230
that's other than, this
is just climate change.

01:14:28.230 --> 01:14:30.000
No, it has nothing to do
with forest mismanagement.

01:14:30.000 --> 01:14:33.290
That's you, it's not California,
those are national forests.

01:14:34.931 --> 01:14:36.860
I'm not gonna get into the details of it,

01:14:36.860 --> 01:14:39.910
but the thing that
really just kind of like,

01:14:39.910 --> 01:14:42.173
I could not go back to
sleep is I was like,

01:14:43.168 --> 01:14:46.180
and it was related to some of the stuff

01:14:46.180 --> 01:14:47.890
that I was reading about

01:14:47.890 --> 01:14:52.443
in the way that we dig in so deeply

01:14:53.810 --> 01:14:55.630
and identify with our position

01:14:55.630 --> 01:15:00.160
and we think of ourselves as
Democrats and Republicans,

01:15:00.160 --> 01:15:02.560
Christians, atheists, whatever,

01:15:02.560 --> 01:15:06.003
and we get into our group and we just,

01:15:07.220 --> 01:15:12.220
we put these shields around us

01:15:12.670 --> 01:15:14.910
and those shields cannot be penetrated

01:15:14.910 --> 01:15:16.830
by anybody else's ideas.

01:15:16.830 --> 01:15:17.663
Yeah.

01:15:17.663 --> 01:15:19.310
And I mean, I really think that it's,

01:15:21.810 --> 01:15:23.223
it will be our downfall.

01:15:24.710 --> 01:15:29.710
The problems that we face
as a species are so large

01:15:33.510 --> 01:15:37.900
and there's so many of us
that our level of division,

01:15:37.900 --> 01:15:39.690
it will be our downfall.

01:15:39.690 --> 01:15:42.106
Well, that's the hopeful
conclusion I was waiting for.

01:15:42.106 --> 01:15:44.530
Well, no, and I was just like,

01:15:44.530 --> 01:15:46.430
wow, I can't stop thinking about this.

01:15:48.840 --> 01:15:52.023
But related to that,
as I was driving back,

01:15:52.880 --> 01:15:54.850
and again, this is related
to a book that I was reading

01:15:54.850 --> 01:15:57.070
which I'm not going to talk
about what book I was reading

01:15:57.070 --> 01:15:58.890
and that kind of made me
think about these things,

01:15:58.890 --> 01:16:01.213
'cause maybe I'll talk about
that at a different time.

01:16:02.640 --> 01:16:06.190
But I thought about our conversation,

01:16:06.190 --> 01:16:08.960
the conversation that we
had about me believing

01:16:08.960 --> 01:16:11.180
that you were as competitive as me.

01:16:11.180 --> 01:16:14.343
Now, I'll say I have looked at
what people have said about,

01:16:15.200 --> 01:16:16.370
a few people have talked about

01:16:16.370 --> 01:16:17.630
what they thought about the conversation

01:16:17.630 --> 01:16:20.480
and kind of analyzed,
thank you for those of you

01:16:20.480 --> 01:16:22.580
who have contributed to that conversation.

01:16:23.670 --> 01:16:24.870
I mean, I definitely got the impression

01:16:24.870 --> 01:16:25.703
that a lot of people were like,

01:16:25.703 --> 01:16:28.300
why did you guys end up
talking about that for an hour?

01:16:28.300 --> 01:16:31.180
And the reason we ended up
talking about that for an hour

01:16:31.180 --> 01:16:34.232
is because of me, because I
thought that it was gonna be,

01:16:34.232 --> 01:16:37.210
you know, I never intended
for us to talk about,

01:16:37.210 --> 01:16:38.700
I thought I was gonna say,

01:16:38.700 --> 01:16:40.130
I think you're just as competitive as me

01:16:40.130 --> 01:16:42.220
and we'd have a 10 minute
conversation about it

01:16:42.220 --> 01:16:43.640
and it would be lighthearted

01:16:43.640 --> 01:16:48.083
and it would end somewhere
where you'd be like, yeah, I am.

01:16:50.480 --> 01:16:54.903
But the moment that you
were like, I don't think so.

01:16:56.372 --> 01:17:01.372
I was like, okay, well, I do think so.

01:17:01.500 --> 01:17:05.773
And it became this hour
long conversation about it.

01:17:07.210 --> 01:17:09.923
And basically what I realized is that,

01:17:11.244 --> 01:17:12.720
and this is, I mean, this is my tendency.

01:17:12.720 --> 01:17:17.720
My tendency is to have an
opinion, have a perspective,

01:17:20.380 --> 01:17:25.380
and then be very invested in
getting whoever I'm talking to

01:17:25.570 --> 01:17:27.933
to agree with my perspective, right.

01:17:30.630 --> 01:17:33.590
And one of the things that kind of clicked

01:17:33.590 --> 01:17:36.310
based on some stuff that I was reading

01:17:36.310 --> 01:17:40.540
is that all arguments, all debate,

01:17:40.540 --> 01:17:44.430
all sort of obsession
with wanting to be right,

01:17:44.430 --> 01:17:49.210
is based in an overidentification
with our mental position.

01:17:49.210 --> 01:17:53.900
In other words, I become invested

01:17:53.900 --> 01:17:56.770
in this opinion that I
have, which by the way,

01:17:56.770 --> 01:17:59.250
is not even something
that I really care about.

01:17:59.250 --> 01:18:01.200
Whether or not you're competitive

01:18:01.200 --> 01:18:04.460
or whether or not you see
how you are as competitive

01:18:04.460 --> 01:18:05.880
is not an important thing.

01:18:05.880 --> 01:18:08.840
This is not something that
impacts my life, your life,

01:18:08.840 --> 01:18:11.330
this is just a conversation,

01:18:11.330 --> 01:18:15.743
almost a philosophical
inconsequential debate of kind.

01:18:17.670 --> 01:18:20.410
But I was invested proving to you

01:18:24.980 --> 01:18:27.510
that you actually think the same way I do.

01:18:27.510 --> 01:18:31.280
And that's based in ego.

01:18:31.280 --> 01:18:35.690
And it's based in me identifying
myself with my position,

01:18:35.690 --> 01:18:39.280
but also ultimately, my fear of death.

01:18:39.280 --> 01:18:40.920
And let me explain that.
Okay.

01:18:40.920 --> 01:18:42.470
This is kind of mind blowing.

01:18:44.160 --> 01:18:48.810
But basically, you know, one
of the humanity's big problems

01:18:48.810 --> 01:18:51.487
is this overidentification with something

01:18:51.487 --> 01:18:54.160
that is a thought that you have

01:18:54.160 --> 01:18:56.040
and a way that you identify yourself.

01:18:56.040 --> 01:18:57.180
Like I talked about a second ago,

01:18:57.180 --> 01:18:59.330
like this division that is in our country.

01:18:59.330 --> 01:19:04.330
And by having, once you identify
yourself with an opinion,

01:19:04.580 --> 01:19:07.880
you actually translate your own existence

01:19:07.880 --> 01:19:12.880
into that opinion being
maintained and spread.

01:19:13.866 --> 01:19:16.130
And that's where it gets into this like,

01:19:16.130 --> 01:19:18.590
trying to protect your own existence.

01:19:18.590 --> 01:19:20.070
So what I'm saying is, is that

01:19:20.070 --> 01:19:23.340
I identify myself with this argument,

01:19:23.340 --> 01:19:26.010
because I feel like if I can
get you to agree with me,

01:19:26.010 --> 01:19:28.840
if I can get you to
think like I do on this,

01:19:28.840 --> 01:19:30.380
then that kind of validates me,

01:19:30.380 --> 01:19:32.300
it validates my own existence.

01:19:32.300 --> 01:19:35.020
And if I'm not able to do
that and I've identified,

01:19:35.020 --> 01:19:37.510
I've placed some of my
own personal identity

01:19:37.510 --> 01:19:39.650
in that position, if
that position isn't true

01:19:39.650 --> 01:19:42.093
there's a little bit less than me around.

01:19:43.170 --> 01:19:44.620
It's a fascinating concept.

01:19:44.620 --> 01:19:47.150
You're saying you discovered
this about yourself

01:19:47.150 --> 01:19:48.280
more so than you're saying,

01:19:48.280 --> 01:19:50.630
I discovered how everybody operates.

01:19:50.630 --> 01:19:52.640
I think I had this realization

01:19:52.640 --> 01:19:53.690
that both things were happening,

01:19:53.690 --> 01:19:55.330
because the first thing that happened

01:19:55.330 --> 01:19:58.610
is this overwhelming like sense of burden

01:19:58.610 --> 01:20:00.640
about how divided the country is.

01:20:00.640 --> 01:20:04.290
And being really mad about
the country being divided.

01:20:04.290 --> 01:20:07.610
And also having a very
interesting perspective

01:20:07.610 --> 01:20:08.540
that I bring to that

01:20:08.540 --> 01:20:10.360
because I'm somebody that's been on

01:20:10.360 --> 01:20:13.060
both sides of the political
spectrum in my adult life.

01:20:14.440 --> 01:20:16.980
So I feel like I have an
appreciation for both sides,

01:20:16.980 --> 01:20:19.450
it's like, I know how conservatives think

01:20:19.450 --> 01:20:20.720
and I know how liberals think

01:20:20.720 --> 01:20:23.400
because I've had very
similar thought patterns

01:20:23.400 --> 01:20:24.263
to both of them.

01:20:26.280 --> 01:20:29.400
But then realizing that on a small scale,

01:20:29.400 --> 01:20:33.240
and maybe an inconsequential
scale, I had demonstrated

01:20:33.240 --> 01:20:35.700
that I'm just as big
a part of the problem,

01:20:35.700 --> 01:20:37.730
just in that little conversation we had.

01:20:37.730 --> 01:20:39.390
Now, I appreciate what people had to say

01:20:39.390 --> 01:20:40.223
when they were like,

01:20:40.223 --> 01:20:41.831
oh, I'm so glad you guys
had that conversation.

01:20:41.831 --> 01:20:44.910
And you're able to have like this conflict

01:20:44.910 --> 01:20:46.420
without being mad at each other

01:20:46.420 --> 01:20:48.720
and you kind of demonstrated
how to have healthy conflict.

01:20:48.720 --> 01:20:50.780
And if that's what came
from it, then great,

01:20:50.780 --> 01:20:52.280
I'm glad we had the conversation.

01:20:52.280 --> 01:20:56.780
But I realize that not
just that conversation,

01:20:56.780 --> 01:20:59.500
but the way that I think in general,

01:20:59.500 --> 01:21:01.380
even when I did my deconstruction story,

01:21:01.380 --> 01:21:03.970
as much as I was trying really hard,

01:21:03.970 --> 01:21:07.710
because I know my
tendency, to be persuasive,

01:21:07.710 --> 01:21:11.620
but just to be expository
and just tell my story.

01:21:11.620 --> 01:21:16.210
I know that I have a tendency,
I want to justify myself.

01:21:16.210 --> 01:21:19.340
I want to validate my own position

01:21:19.340 --> 01:21:23.850
because I've got a big ego.

01:21:23.850 --> 01:21:27.530
And I want to perpetuate my own ideas

01:21:27.530 --> 01:21:32.530
because I have over identified
with ideas as inconsequential

01:21:32.810 --> 01:21:36.170
as thinking that you need to
realize how competitive you are

01:21:37.058 --> 01:21:40.040
and the details of the argument are kinda,

01:21:40.040 --> 01:21:41.210
they don't even matter.

01:21:41.210 --> 01:21:42.683
It just becomes this.

01:21:47.637 --> 01:21:51.540
I just kinda like I dug in and said,

01:21:51.540 --> 01:21:52.760
let's have this conversation

01:21:52.760 --> 01:21:55.450
and hopefully it will end
with you agreeing with me.

01:21:55.450 --> 01:21:57.743
And I think that that is,

01:21:59.310 --> 01:22:01.310
it's a small picture of what's happening

01:22:02.440 --> 01:22:04.820
not just nationwide, but worldwide.

01:22:04.820 --> 01:22:09.333
It's just a preoccupation
with being right.

01:22:10.347 --> 01:22:12.100
You know what I'm saying?

01:22:12.100 --> 01:22:15.380
Identifying with an idea

01:22:15.380 --> 01:22:17.950
and then being validated in that idea.

01:22:17.950 --> 01:22:21.600
Because that validates you as
a person, or people as people.

01:22:21.600 --> 01:22:25.993
And the way that ideology,

01:22:27.420 --> 01:22:31.180
identification with a
particular political party,

01:22:31.180 --> 01:22:33.963
particular worldview,
particular religion, whatever,

01:22:36.605 --> 01:22:40.420
it comes with a whole set of things

01:22:40.420 --> 01:22:43.110
that you have to embrace, right?

01:22:43.110 --> 01:22:44.860
Like how did it ever get to the point

01:22:44.860 --> 01:22:49.860
where identifying as a follower of Jesus

01:22:50.010 --> 01:22:55.010
went hand in hand with being
extremely capitalist, right?

01:22:56.422 --> 01:22:59.090
How did, how did we get to a place

01:23:02.020 --> 01:23:04.560
where identifying yourself
as a follower of Jesus

01:23:04.560 --> 01:23:08.460
meant denying that humans are
causing climate change, right?

01:23:08.460 --> 01:23:11.490
I think we got there
because it's a package,

01:23:11.490 --> 01:23:13.253
you take the package.

01:23:15.751 --> 01:23:17.620
Listen, I'm not judging,

01:23:17.620 --> 01:23:20.440
what I'm saying is that
I'm guilty of it as well.

01:23:20.440 --> 01:23:22.910
I'm beginning to try to explore,

01:23:22.910 --> 01:23:25.590
okay, well, where else
has this manifested itself

01:23:25.590 --> 01:23:28.230
outside of just a dumb argument

01:23:28.230 --> 01:23:29.630
about how competitive you are.

01:23:29.630 --> 01:23:31.560
But ultimately what I'm saying is that,

01:23:31.560 --> 01:23:34.563
we had that conversation and
it became this big thing,

01:23:36.290 --> 01:23:38.050
not even because I thought
that it was important,

01:23:38.050 --> 01:23:40.170
but because I made the statement

01:23:40.170 --> 01:23:42.270
and then I felt like I had to defend it,

01:23:42.270 --> 01:23:44.170
I felt like I had to defend it for an hour

01:23:44.170 --> 01:23:45.320
worth of conversation.

01:23:45.320 --> 01:23:50.000
You know, I think that
that's not the typical,

01:23:50.000 --> 01:23:51.680
that's not how things typically play out

01:23:51.680 --> 01:23:53.800
in our friendship dynamic.

01:23:53.800 --> 01:23:56.520
I think it wasn't what
you expected to happen.

01:23:56.520 --> 01:23:58.120
Again, it was like you thought

01:23:58.120 --> 01:24:00.330
there would be a little
playful conversation

01:24:00.330 --> 01:24:01.910
and then an agreement.

01:24:01.910 --> 01:24:05.750
I think with a lot of things
going through life together,

01:24:05.750 --> 01:24:10.750
there has been this dynamic of, you know,

01:24:10.960 --> 01:24:13.064
if we're exploring an idea or,

01:24:13.064 --> 01:24:17.441
if you're more intent
on exploring an idea,

01:24:17.441 --> 01:24:20.230
then you can bounce it off of me

01:24:20.230 --> 01:24:25.230
and more often than not, I
would explore it with you

01:24:27.200 --> 01:24:29.470
and validate that position.

01:24:29.470 --> 01:24:30.593
Mm-hmm.

01:24:32.046 --> 01:24:36.380
I do think that's an interesting
dynamic that, you know,

01:24:36.380 --> 01:24:37.840
I didn't realize was happening.

01:24:37.840 --> 01:24:42.840
And it was like, if someone
was looking at my life

01:24:44.740 --> 01:24:45.790
they might say, well,

01:24:48.230 --> 01:24:51.600
you just glom on to whatever
Rhett believes or whatever.

01:24:51.600 --> 01:24:55.360
Or, you believe whatever he tells you,

01:24:55.360 --> 01:25:00.360
would be like a severe and
non charitable way to put it,

01:25:00.910 --> 01:25:02.510
'cause that's not actually true.

01:25:04.380 --> 01:25:05.840
But what I never thought of

01:25:05.840 --> 01:25:08.700
is with you like talking about how this,

01:25:08.700 --> 01:25:11.080
'cause I didn't I didn't
know how this worked

01:25:11.080 --> 01:25:15.773
in your own mind, but if
hearing that someone may say,

01:25:18.430 --> 01:25:20.500
Link fulfills a role in your life

01:25:20.500 --> 01:25:25.060
to validate that part of yourself

01:25:25.060 --> 01:25:27.600
that's associated with ideas.

01:25:27.600 --> 01:25:29.420
Potentially.
Potentially.

01:25:29.420 --> 01:25:31.330
Again, I don't know, but it does,

01:25:31.330 --> 01:25:35.503
I do see, I mean, there is
that pattern because I think,

01:25:36.760 --> 01:25:40.240
again, you're exploring
how you interact with ideas

01:25:40.240 --> 01:25:44.880
and identity and again, it's
like, I don't have to say

01:25:44.880 --> 01:25:47.050
I have that exact same experience

01:25:47.050 --> 01:25:51.198
in order to validate your
realization or your confession.

01:25:51.198 --> 01:25:54.170
Or me to say, I don't relate to that.

01:25:54.170 --> 01:25:56.783
It's like, this conversation's
not about me at all.

01:25:56.783 --> 01:26:00.293
It's 'cause I mean, it's
one thing to be like,

01:26:02.930 --> 01:26:05.100
so are you saying you shouldn't
have opinions about things

01:26:05.100 --> 01:26:07.110
or you shouldn't argue for things?

01:26:07.110 --> 01:26:07.943
It's like, well, I mean,

01:26:07.943 --> 01:26:10.470
I think we're in a place in our country

01:26:10.470 --> 01:26:12.800
where I think that there are principles

01:26:12.800 --> 01:26:15.610
and there are people that
need to be advocated for

01:26:15.610 --> 01:26:17.760
and there are things that
need to be stood for.

01:26:17.760 --> 01:26:20.864
So I think that being
a person of principle,

01:26:20.864 --> 01:26:24.200
of character and integrity
and standing for things,

01:26:24.200 --> 01:26:27.942
I'm not saying you shouldn't
have opinions and convictions.

01:26:27.942 --> 01:26:30.430
But we've talked about how we also value,

01:26:30.430 --> 01:26:32.319
I mean, with our deconstruction stories,

01:26:32.319 --> 01:26:34.730
how much we value open-mindedness

01:26:34.730 --> 01:26:39.730
and like not skipping
from one entrenched thing,

01:26:41.044 --> 01:26:45.340
belief system, just to another one.

01:26:45.340 --> 01:26:48.280
But it's less about
where we're entrenched,

01:26:48.280 --> 01:26:50.940
that's a different conversation,
than just talking about

01:26:50.940 --> 01:26:53.840
our value of open-mindedness

01:26:53.840 --> 01:26:57.400
and not ruling anything out in order to.

01:26:57.400 --> 01:27:00.520
Well, and I think that the challenge is

01:27:00.520 --> 01:27:04.860
finding a way to have an
opinion, a belief, a conviction,

01:27:04.860 --> 01:27:09.860
to fight for something, but
not to overly identify with it

01:27:10.330 --> 01:27:12.830
to the point that your self
worth and your self identity

01:27:12.830 --> 01:27:17.283
is based in some position
that you have, right?

01:27:18.440 --> 01:27:20.350
Because at that moment

01:27:20.350 --> 01:27:23.320
you become more committed to your position

01:27:23.320 --> 01:27:27.302
than you do to the human
race, to the greater good.

01:27:27.302 --> 01:27:31.480
That's a selfish, it actually
becomes a selfish pursuit.

01:27:31.480 --> 01:27:34.450
And I just, in that
podcast I demonstrated how

01:27:35.930 --> 01:27:40.280
that can happen with
something so unimportant

01:27:40.280 --> 01:27:42.870
as me thinking that you're competitive.

01:27:42.870 --> 01:27:47.660
Like, what if it was having
an argument about abortion?

01:27:47.660 --> 01:27:49.350
You know, for God's sake, right.

01:27:49.350 --> 01:27:51.350
But no, I wasn't even
talking about something

01:27:51.350 --> 01:27:52.940
that has any emotional weight to it,

01:27:52.940 --> 01:27:55.720
I was just talking about whether
or not you're competitive.

01:27:55.720 --> 01:27:57.930
It came from a conversation
about board games,

01:27:57.930 --> 01:28:01.950
but yet even then I was willing to,

01:28:01.950 --> 01:28:04.130
and I was a very willing participant

01:28:04.130 --> 01:28:05.820
in identifying with that position

01:28:05.820 --> 01:28:06.870
and once I threw it out there

01:28:06.870 --> 01:28:10.130
and it encountered some resistant energy,

01:28:10.130 --> 01:28:12.700
I energized the side of my conversation

01:28:12.700 --> 01:28:15.540
to try to get my energy
to beat your energy

01:28:15.540 --> 01:28:19.550
so I could be like my energy
wins and I won this argument.

01:28:19.550 --> 01:28:22.208
And I just feel like that negative energy

01:28:22.208 --> 01:28:24.380
that's happening on both sides,

01:28:24.380 --> 01:28:27.593
it's happening on both sides
of the spectrum at this point,

01:28:29.740 --> 01:28:32.010
is like, there's just
something that's been lost.

01:28:32.010 --> 01:28:36.550
And I'm a part of it, I'm
saying that I am a part of it.

01:28:36.550 --> 01:28:40.170
But I'm realizing that I want
to be a part of the solution

01:28:40.170 --> 01:28:42.930
and not just contribute to the noise.

01:28:42.930 --> 01:28:44.960
I don't really know what that looks like.

01:28:44.960 --> 01:28:46.483
I don't have any application.

01:28:51.453 --> 01:28:54.020
And ultimately, I'm not encouraged,

01:28:54.020 --> 01:28:56.370
so I'm not gonna end it on a happy note.

01:28:56.370 --> 01:28:57.910
For me personally I'm encouraged

01:28:57.910 --> 01:28:59.860
with some of the things that have happened

01:28:59.860 --> 01:29:02.893
and that one of the
things is like, oh man,

01:29:07.810 --> 01:29:12.060
you tend to want to debate or
argue or you want to be right

01:29:12.060 --> 01:29:17.060
and that's not about wanting
people to understand the truth

01:29:18.400 --> 01:29:21.763
as much as it's about you
wanting to be right, your ego.

01:29:22.600 --> 01:29:24.800
I'm glad that I'm
beginning to realize that.

01:29:25.710 --> 01:29:29.560
But the thing that is discouraging

01:29:29.560 --> 01:29:34.560
is our interconnectedness that
we've been able to achieve

01:29:34.650 --> 01:29:39.160
given the proliferation of
the internet has backfired.

01:29:42.060 --> 01:29:46.750
and we're way less connected
I think, than we've ever been.

01:29:46.750 --> 01:29:48.803
And it does not seem to be,

01:29:51.390 --> 01:29:54.240
there doesn't seem to be any
sort of resolution in sight.

01:29:55.620 --> 01:29:58.530
And instead of commenting on that

01:29:58.530 --> 01:30:00.660
I'll take an aside and just say,

01:30:00.660 --> 01:30:02.810
since this is the time
where we're talking about,

01:30:02.810 --> 01:30:05.520
if this is the followup
to the competition,

01:30:05.520 --> 01:30:09.670
I'll just give like the concise
way that I've processed it.

01:30:09.670 --> 01:30:14.670
I think for me, I didn't
read a lot of the comments.

01:30:20.630 --> 01:30:24.233
I had discussions with,
you know, with Christie,

01:30:25.671 --> 01:30:28.220
I might've had a therapy
discussion about it,

01:30:28.220 --> 01:30:29.053
I may not have.

01:30:30.310 --> 01:30:34.330
But one of the things that kinda,

01:30:34.330 --> 01:30:36.470
when I look back on the conversation now

01:30:36.470 --> 01:30:40.580
is the thing that I carry with me is,

01:30:40.580 --> 01:30:44.850
I realized that it was, you know,

01:30:44.850 --> 01:30:49.850
unpacking like the competitive,
your competitive nature.

01:30:51.247 --> 01:30:53.990
You know, I very much internalized that

01:30:53.990 --> 01:30:57.363
as kind of, as a threat, right.

01:30:58.720 --> 01:31:02.350
And then what I,

01:31:02.350 --> 01:31:04.060
part of my process was
kind of coming through that

01:31:04.060 --> 01:31:05.370
and saying, you know what?

01:31:05.370 --> 01:31:09.000
Analyzing that but then also realizing

01:31:10.287 --> 01:31:13.860
that if the thing that sticks with me,

01:31:13.860 --> 01:31:17.165
the positive thing from
the conversation is that

01:31:17.165 --> 01:31:22.090
if there's a competitive spirit between us

01:31:22.090 --> 01:31:25.936
and it doesn't just go one
way, and I'm not denying that,

01:31:25.936 --> 01:31:29.120
I'm not saying I'm not competitive at all.

01:31:29.120 --> 01:31:31.690
But I am asserting that I am not the same,

01:31:31.690 --> 01:31:34.053
I'm not the same as you in this way.

01:31:36.430 --> 01:31:41.300
That I think what it kind of hurt

01:31:41.300 --> 01:31:43.323
because I felt threatened by it.

01:31:44.700 --> 01:31:48.740
The turn was actually being honored by it.

01:31:48.740 --> 01:31:51.680
Like if you, you're not
competitive with everybody,

01:31:51.680 --> 01:31:55.530
if there is a competitive
spirit from you towards me,

01:31:55.530 --> 01:31:59.523
then I can take, I honestly
take that as a compliment.

01:32:00.460 --> 01:32:03.950
That is a level of respect

01:32:03.950 --> 01:32:08.440
that I didn't immediately experience,

01:32:08.440 --> 01:32:11.070
but the more I thought about
it and reflected on it,

01:32:11.070 --> 01:32:14.040
I was like, that's my takeaway from it.

01:32:14.040 --> 01:32:16.593
So it's, you know, I
take it as a compliment.

01:32:17.650 --> 01:32:18.483
Okay, good.

01:32:19.540 --> 01:32:23.950
And yeah, I do find it fascinating that

01:32:23.950 --> 01:32:25.440
because there's mics in front of us

01:32:25.440 --> 01:32:28.230
it changes the dynamic of the
conversation a little bit,

01:32:28.230 --> 01:32:31.680
but the conversation that we had afterward

01:32:31.680 --> 01:32:35.340
for the next two hours, you know,

01:32:35.340 --> 01:32:37.470
there was a lot of good
that came from that too,

01:32:37.470 --> 01:32:40.710
that, you know, didn't need to be on mic

01:32:40.710 --> 01:32:45.710
and I think it was a milestone
moment, and a good thing.

01:32:48.430 --> 01:32:50.180
I'm glad we had the conversation.

01:32:51.730 --> 01:32:53.190
I just have come to grips

01:32:53.190 --> 01:32:56.210
with why I push things the way that I did.

01:32:56.210 --> 01:32:57.500
And some people were really, you know,

01:32:57.500 --> 01:33:02.293
some people really,
really picked up on that.

01:33:03.710 --> 01:33:08.163
And ironically, or I guess not
so ironically, but fittingly,

01:33:09.410 --> 01:33:11.770
even my competitive
nature in a lot of ways

01:33:11.770 --> 01:33:16.540
is based in the way that how I identify,

01:33:16.540 --> 01:33:19.410
what I identify with and what
I find my worth in, right.

01:33:19.410 --> 01:33:23.833
And a lot of times I find my
worth in winning at things.

01:33:24.680 --> 01:33:29.680
And again, that's just another
displacement of my identity

01:33:30.170 --> 01:33:33.050
into this performance,
meeting some expectation,

01:33:33.050 --> 01:33:35.503
whether it's set by me
or set by somebody else.

01:33:37.825 --> 01:33:40.070
So even the argument that I was having

01:33:40.070 --> 01:33:42.372
was trying to get you to realize

01:33:42.372 --> 01:33:45.185
that you think about this
in the same way that I do.

01:33:45.185 --> 01:33:47.720
The thing I was trying
to get you to think about

01:33:47.720 --> 01:33:49.430
was also a symptom of my ego

01:33:49.430 --> 01:33:51.980
and the way I was doing it
was a symptom of my ego.

01:33:54.860 --> 01:33:57.450
And none of this is like
super revolutionary,

01:33:57.450 --> 01:33:59.900
it isn't like if you had a told me that

01:33:59.900 --> 01:34:02.290
before that conversation, I
probably would've been like,

01:34:02.290 --> 01:34:04.560
yeah, that's true, wanting to be right

01:34:04.560 --> 01:34:05.720
and wanting to have an argument

